zed, find the value of $x.$\n\n![](images/68/6821176223c59c981ac57cfa754044a145754b747baf7985f7eefd073dcd8758.png)" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186100648 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV = x\\cdot x\\cdot y = x^2 y\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The surface area is $4 \\, \\textrm{m} ^ 2$. So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} S &= \\textrm{(area of the base)} + 4 \\, \\textrm{(area of one side)} \\\\[5pt] 4 &= x^2 + 4 x y. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{x} - \\dfrac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{x} - \\dfrac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nin the expression $V=x^2y$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} V(x) &= x^2 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x} - \\dfrac{x}{4}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &= x - \\dfrac{x^3}{4}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation $V'(x)=0$. Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\nV'(x) = 1 - \\dfrac{3x^2}{4}\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} V'(x) &= 0 \\\\[5pt] 1 - \\dfrac{3x^2}{4} &=0 \\\\[5pt] x^2 &= \\dfrac{4}{3} \\\\[5pt] x &= \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the side length $x$ must be positive.\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\nis a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\nV''(x) = -\\dfrac{3}{2}x \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad V''\\left(\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right) = -\\sqrt{3} < 0\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nV''\\left(\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nis negative, we conclude that\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\nis indeed a maximum of $V(x)$.\n\nTherefore, the box with the largest possible volume has side length\n\n$$\nx= \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,\\mathrm{m}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186100648 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV = x\\cdot x\\cdot y = x^2 y\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The surface area is $4 \\, \\textrm{m} ^ 2$. So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n S &= \\textrm{(area of the base) } + 4 \\, \\textrm{(area of one side) } \\[5pt]\n4 &= x^2 + 4 x y.}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation: \n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{x} - \\dfrac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y = \\dfrac{1}{x} - \\dfrac{x}{4}$ in the expression $V=x^2y,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nV(x) &= x^2 \\left( \\dfrac{1}{x} - \\dfrac{x}{4}\\right) \\[5pt]\n&= x - \\dfrac{x^3}{4}.\n}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation $V'(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\nV'(x) = 1 - \\dfrac{3x^2}{4},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nV'(x) &= 0 \\[5pt]\n1 - \\dfrac{3x^2}{4} &=0 \\[5pt]\nx^2 &= \\dfrac{4}{3} \\[5pt]\nx &= \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the side length $x$ must be positive. \n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\nV''(x) = -\\dfrac{3}{2}x \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad V''\\left(\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right) = -\\sqrt{3} < 0.\n$$\n\nSince $V''\\left(\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)$ is negative, we conclude that $x=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(x).$\n\nTherefore, the box with the largest possible volume has side length $x= \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,\\mathrm{m}.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186100649 1071 "Consider a box with a square base with side lengths $x$ and height $y$, as shown below. The volume of the box is $1$ cubic unit. Given that the surface area of the box has been minimized, find the value of $x$.\n\n![](images/5f/5f852cff121a53206cdcfeb8f4a0efec5a43fd2ed73ab569ec537cbe896b405c.png)" 13194139535463 17592186045417 f