m{m}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the hall is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= a \\cdot b = 4.5 \\cdot 6 = 27m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186105537 1074 "The area of a rectangle is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = a \\cdot b,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are the lengths of the sides.\n\nFrom the figure, we obtain that \n\n$$\na = 4.5\\,\\mathrm{m}, \\qquad b = 6\\,\\mathrm{m}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the area of the hall is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =a\\cdot b=4.5\\cdot 6=27\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186105538 1071 "The area of the rectangular field shown below is $2\\,400\\,\\textrm{m}^2$. What is the value of $x$?\n\n![](images/94/9419ae4a3099216109bff99906e5d1724a8f01f61e48e22edc62c0e1b62a1bef.png)" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186105538 1071 "The area of the rectangular field shown below is $2\\,400\\,\\textrm{m}^2.$ What is the value of $x?$\n\n![](images/94/9419ae4a3099216109bff99906e5d1724a8f01f61e48e22edc62c0e1b62a1bef.png)" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186105538 1074 "The area of a rectangle is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = a \\cdot b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are the lengths of the sides.\n\nFrom the figure, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = 2\\,400\\mathrm{m}^2, \\qquad a = 60\\,\\mathrm{m}, \\qquad b=x\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= a \\cdot b \\\\\n2400 &= 60 \\cdot x \\\\\nx &= \\frac{2400}{60} \\\\\nx &= 40m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186105538 1074 "The area of a rectangle is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = a \\cdot b,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are the lengths of the sides.\n\nFrom the figure, we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = 2\\,400\\mathrm{m}^2, \\qquad a = 60\\,\\mathrm{m}, \\qquad b=x .\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =a\\cdot b \\\\ 2\\,400 & =60\\cdot x \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\,400}{60} \\\\ x & =40\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186105671 1071 "Find the area of the rectangle $ABCD$ if its perimeter is $40$ and ${BC} = 12$." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186105671 1071 "Find the area of the rectangle $ABCD$ if its perimeter is $40$ and ${BC} = 12.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186105671 1074 "We can find the length of ${AB}$ by using the formula for the perimeter, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= AB + BC + CD + DA \\\\\np &= 2AB + 2BC \\\\\n40 &= 2AB + 2(12) \\\\\n40 &= 2AB + 24 \\\\\n2AB &= 16 \\\\\nAB &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the rectangle $ABCD$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= AB \\cdot BC \\\\\n&= 8 \\cdot 12 \\\\\n&= 96\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186105671 1074 "We can find the length of ${AB}$ by using the formula for the perimeter, as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AB+BC+CD+DA \\\\ p & =2AB+2BC \\\\ 40 & =2AB+2(12) \\\\ 40 & =2AB+24 \\\\ 2AB & =16 \\\\ AB & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the rectangle $ABCD$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =AB\\cdot BC \\\\  & =8\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =96.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186105838 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508923 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186105838 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508962 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186105870 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508965 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186105870 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508973 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186105872 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508991 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186105872 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508996 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186105930 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508961 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186105930 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508971 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186105932 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508966 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186105932 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508986 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186105935 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508939 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186105987 1050 420.0 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186106016 1050 420.0 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186106416 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186106416 1071 "![](images/91/91a554b467201b7507b531dcc0b6e1d5d9970d3e6d6554878644a31e5e38d9fa.png)\n\nThe area of the rectangular field shown above is $500\\,{\\text{yd}}^{2}.$ What is the value of $x?$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186106416 1071 "![](images/91/91a554b467201b7507b531dcc0b6e1d5d9970d3e6d6554878644a31e5e38d9fa.png)\n\nThe area of the rectangular field shown above is $500yd^{2}$. What is the value of $x$?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186106416 1074 "The area of a rectangle is given by \n\n$$\nA=a\\cdot b,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are the lengths of the sides.\n\nFrom the figure, we obtain that \n\n$$\na=20\\,yd,\\,A=500\\,{yd}^{2},\\,b=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =a\\cdot b \\\\ 500 & =20\\cdot x \\\\ x & =\\frac{500}{20} \\\\ x & =25\\,\\text{yd}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186107049 1064 "The **area** of a shape is a measure of how much space is enclosed by a shape on a plane. Let's consider a square with sides of length of $1\\,\\textrm{in}$.\n\n![](images/bc/bc59b6e5a60a2f22e6c7fb102eba2f255dd09e115fd04a16050570f9f8b053f4.png)\n\nThe area of this square is equal to ${\\color{red}{1\\,\\textrm{in}^2}}$, which we say in words as \"one square inch.\"\n\nSimilarly, if we have three squares with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{cm}$, $1\\,\\textrm{m}$, and $1\\,\\textrm{ft}$ respectively, then their respective areas will be\n$1\\,\\textrm{cm}^2, \\qquad 1\\,\\textrm{m}^2, \\qquad 1\\,\\textrm{ft}^2$." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186107049 1064 "The **area** of a shape is a measure of how much space is enclosed by a shape on a plane. Let's consider a square with sides of length of $1\\,\\textrm{in}.$\n\n![](images/bc/bc59b6e5a60a2f22e6c7fb102eba2f255dd09e115fd04a16050570f9f8b053f4.png)\n\nThe area of this square is equal to ${\\color{red}{1\\,\\textrm{in}^2}},$ which we say in words as \"one square inch.\"\n\nSimilarly, if we have three squares with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{cm},$ $1\\,\\textrm{m},$ and $1\\,\\textrm{ft}$ respectively, then their respective areas will be \n\n$$\n1\\,\\textrm{cm}^2, \\qquad\n\t1\\,\\textrm{m}^2, \\qquad \n\t1\\,\\textrm{ft}^2.\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186107050 1064 "Let's now suppose that we would like to compute the area of the square $ABCD$, shown below. Notice that, this time, the sides of the square span multiple unit lengths.\n\n![](images/14/1441893036f9a41ed0daa8c8ce078d6a733bab5bb88dc60259a6b9ebc1c2e7c0.png)\n\nThe length of one side of a grid square is equal to $1\\,\\textrm{cm}$. So, the area of one grid square is $1\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$.\n\nNow, we need to find how many grid squares are contained inside $ABCD$.\n\n![](images/5f/5f293762b05013689996298a7804fb5f507eb228112397641fb8f662ff74ba49.png)\n\nInside the large blue square, there are $3$ rows containing $3$ grid squares each, so we can fit exactly\n\n$$\n3 \\times 3 = 3^2 = 9\n$$\n\ngrid squares inside $ABCD$.\n\nEach grid square has area $1\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$, so the area $\\mathcal A$ of $ABCD$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = (3\\,\\textrm{cm})^2 = 9\\,\\textrm{cm}^2\n$$\n\nIn general, if we have a square with a side of length $a$, then the area of the square is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = a^2\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186107050 1064 "Let's now suppose that we would like to compute the area of the square $ABCD,$ shown below. Notice that, this time, the sides of the square span multiple unit lengths.\n\n![](images/14/1441893036f9a41ed0daa8c8ce078d6a733bab5bb88dc60259a6b9ebc1c2e7c0.png)\n\nThe length of one side of a grid square is equal to $1\\,\\textrm{cm}.$ So, the area of one grid square is $1\\,\\textrm{cm}^2.$ \n\nNow, we need to find how many grid squares are contained inside $ABCD.$\n\n![](images/5f/5f293762b05013689996298a7804fb5f507eb228112397641fb8f662ff74ba49.png)\n\nInside the large blue square, there are $3$ rows containing $3$ grid squares each, so we can fit exactly $3 \\times 3 = 3^2 = 9$ grid squares inside $ABCD.$\n\nEach grid square has area $1\\,\\textrm{cm}^2,$ so the area $\\mathcal A$ of $ABCD$ is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = (3\\,\\textrm{cm})^2 = 9\\,\\textrm{cm}^2.\n$$\n\nIn general, if we have a square with a side of length $a$, then the area of the square is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = a^2.\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186107052 1064 "In general, for a rectangle with sides of length $a$ and $b$, the area of the rectangle is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = a \\cdot b\n$$\n\nLet's demonstrate by finding the area of the rectangle $PQRS$, shown below.\n\n![](images/a0/a0890441b4c7e888ca7ede71a075825ca6700f79d242d1dec7e35ab2d787e5e0.png)\n\nFrom the figure, we see that the rectangle has the following side lengths:\n\n$$\na = {PQ} = 10\\,\\mathrm{cm}, \\qquad b = {PS} = 6\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= a \\cdot b \\\\\n&= 10cm \\cdot 6cm \\\\\n&= 60cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt does not matter which side we call $a$ and which side we call $b$. In the above example, if we set\n\n$$\na = {QR} = 6\\,\\mathrm{cm}, \\qquad b = {RS} = 10\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$\n\nthen we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= a \\cdot b \\\\\n&= 10cm \\cdot 6cm \\\\\n&= 60cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas before." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186107052 1064 "In general, for a rectangle with sides of length $a$ and $b,$ the area of the rectangle is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = a \\cdot b.\n$$\n\nLet's demonstrate by finding the area of the rectangle $PQRS,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a0/a0890441b4c7e888ca7ede71a075825ca6700f79d242d1dec7e35ab2d787e5e0.png)\n\nFrom the figure, we see that the rectangle has the following side lengths:\n\n$$\na = {PQ} = 10\\,\\mathrm{cm},\n\t\\qquad\n\tb = {PS} = 6\\,\\mathrm{cm}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =a\\cdot b \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{cm}\\cdot 6\\,\\text{cm} \\\\  & =60\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt does not matter which side we call $a$ and which side we call $b.$ In the above example, if we set\n\n$$\na = {QR} = 6\\,\\mathrm{cm},\n\\qquad\nb = {RS} = 10\\,\\mathrm{cm},\n$$\n\n \n\nthen we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =a\\cdot b \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{cm}\\cdot 6\\,\\text{cm} \\\\  & =60\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas before." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186114948 1071 "The value of a certain stock, in dollars, decays according to the formula\n\n$$\nV(t)= 4\\, 500\\cdot (0.85)^t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the stock was bought. \n\nI. What does $V(3)$ mean in this context?\n\nII. Calculate $V(2) - V(3),$ and determine its meaning in this context." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186114948 1071 "The value of a certain stock, in dollars, decays according to the formula\n\n$V(t)= 4\\, 500\\cdot (0.85)^t$,\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the stock was bought.\n\n1. What does $V(3)$ mean in this context?\n2. Calculate $V(2) - V(3)$, and determine its meaning in this context." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186114948 1074 "- The value $V(3)$ represents the value of the function $V(t)$ at $t=3$. In this context, it represents the value of the stock $3$ years after the initial investment.\n- By similar reasoning to the above, $V(2)$ represents the stock's value $2$ years after the initial investment. Therefore, the expression\n$V(2) - V(3)$\nrepresents the total reduction in the value of the stock between $t=2$ and $t=3$ years since the initial investment.Now, let's compute its value. We get\n$V(2) - V(3)|= 4500 \\cdot (0.85)^{2} - 4500 \\cdot (0.85)^{3}; = 487.6874; \\approx 488$,\nrounded to the nearest integer. Therefore, the value of the stock dropped by $\\[math]488$ between years $2$ and $3$." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186114948 1074 "- The value $V(3)$ represents the value of the function $V(t)$ at $t=3.$ In this context, it represents the value of the stock $3$ years after the initial investment.\n\n- By similar reasoning to the above, $V(2)$ represents the stock's value $2$ years after the initial investment. Therefore, the expression\n\n$$\nV(2) - V(3)\n$$\n\nrepresents the total reduction in the value of the stock between $t=2$ and $t=3$ years since the initial investment.\n\nNow, let's compute its value. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(2)-V(3) & =4\\,500\\cdot (0.85)^{2}-4\\,500\\cdot (0.85)^{3} \\\\  & =487.6874 \\\\  & ≈488,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer. Therefore, the value of the stock dropped by $\\[math]488$ between years $2$ and $3.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186114949 1071 "The market value ![](images/93/93636eb5ea1d6d8df4d0390651023dd9a4aa32ff9077d3edca03c6a4d2593c9f.svg) of a car, in dollars, ![](images/70/707bb132def3690d81bf1f0c8cf416c1382322ab19d66264cdab1decc4669307.svg) years since it was first purchased depreciates according to the following rule: \n![](images/9e/9eab3a81e5055a62b6e3dbba458b267fadf4ebdfe44786f23259fabc3d1cd409.svg)\n\nWhat does the base of the exponential expression ![](images/d6/d660f48227bd14736021adeb26de738a61fe583d94bc205ead8a5bab1c1acb61.svg) mean in this context?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186114949 1071 "The market value $V(t)$ of a car, in dollars, $t$ years since it was first purchased depreciates according to the following rule:\n$V(t) = 25\\, 000\\cdot (0.95)^t$\n\nWhat does the base of the exponential expression $(0.95)$ mean in this context?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186114949 1074 "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form ![](images/52/527f98e7ede20d48ea37c58afcb0d4489fa5ad6651f21e3a7d1c08f5e3bb76ac.svg) where\n\n- ![](images/b6/b6cceb36174e4439694c8b988c00019d9707a7b6377e0c806c51c8d330e0ab4b.svg) represents the initial value of the car (in dollars).\n\n- ![](images/ef/ef47ff747a4899500bf1cdaa584e477954cd417238e8b7e695862da9b0022c6a.svg) where ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) is the decay rate.\n\n- ![](images/70/707bb132def3690d81bf1f0c8cf416c1382322ab19d66264cdab1decc4669307.svg) is the time since the car was first purchased, in years.\n\nSolving for the decay rate ![](images/22/22d9393dcae7cd46f7da8986fc4764e2f14e127fb9dcdf114770ad2de2f6b098.svg) we get\n\n![](images/64/64161e1a2f19b6bdb3f7651e8a553179126ccf33901d20311347dbd9ff98aaab.svg)\n\nNow, ![](images/85/85eb3227c042f8ec4de535b2fb7fc37c5cd34735961e5ba26e6a511eff5707b5.svg) (expressed as a percent) is equal to ![](images/92/92e44f079247c93011878456e1294adc24fe51c089fd07e12107f1ad75e2d91d.svg) \n\nSo, the base of the exponential expression ![](images/55/551172a22e640525b7b69ef43cab353b50dd3ca6123574a3dd5df79475ea5496.svg) means that the value of the car depreciates by ![](images/41/414c7a992cb07b0cf60bef2446dc3eb4072aeb99acedf6d77c02b5c183cdfc63.svg) each year." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186114949 1074 "We're asked for the decay rate, so we use the decay rate formula.\n\nThe given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form\n\n$$\ny(t) = a \\cdot (1-r)^t\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a=25\\,000$ represents the initial value of the car (in dollars).\n- $1-r = 0.95$, where $r$ is the decay rate.\n- $t$ is the time since the car was first purchased, in years.\n\nSolving for the decay rate $r$, we get\n\n$$\n1-r = 0.95\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad r = 0.05\n$$\n\nNow, $0.05$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $5\\%$. Therefore, the value of the car depreciates by $5\\%$ each year." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186114950 1071 "A house was purchased for $\\[math]90\\,000$. Shortly after purchasing the property, the owners discovered widening cracks in the foundation. As a result, the value of the property is reduced by one-half every year. What does the expression $\\[/math]\\,90\\,000\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^6$ mean in this context?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186114950 1071 "A house was purchased for $\\[math]90\\,000.$ Shortly after purchasing the property, the owners discovered widening cracks in the foundation. As a result, the value of the property is reduced by one-half every year. What does the expression $\\[/math]\\,90\\,000\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^6$ mean in this context?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186114950 1074 "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form\n\n$$\ny(t) = a \\cdot b^t\n$$\n\nwhere we have the following:\n\n- $a=90\\,000$ represents the purchase price of the property.\n- $b = \\dfrac 1 2$ is the decay factor.\n- $t = 6$ the time, in years, since the property was bought.\n\nSince $t=6$, the expression\n\n$$\n\\[math]\\,90\\,000\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^6\n$$\n\ngives the value of the property after $6$ years." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186114950 1074 "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form $y(t) = a \\cdot b^t,$ where we have the following:\n\n- $a=90\\,000$ represents the purchase price of the property.\n\n- $b = \\dfrac 1 2$ is the decay factor.\n\n- $t = 6$ the time, in years, since the property was bought.\n\nSince $t=6,$ the expression $\\[math]\\,90\\,000\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^6$ gives the value of the property after $6$ years." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186115383 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508972 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186115383 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508980 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186115384 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508947 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186115384 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508951 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186115385 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508950 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186115385 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508967 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186115438 1050 420.0 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186116434 1064 "In this lesson, we will learn how to interpret various aspects of an exponential decay expression or function.\n\nFor example, suppose that, due to overfishing, a fish population decays exponentially. The population $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = 5\\,000 \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^t\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in months, since the overfishing began. What can we deduce about the population of fish from this model?\n\nThe first thing to notice is that the function above is an exponential decay function of the form\n\n$$\ny(t) = a \\cdot b^t\n$$\n\nSo, we can deduce the following:\n\n- $a=5\\,000$ represents the initial value. So this tells us that there were $5\\,000$ fish when the overfishing began.\n- $b = \\dfrac 1 2$ is the decay factor. Since $0 < b < 1$, this tells us that we're dealing with exponential decay, *not* exponential growth (for exponential growth we need $b > 1$).\n- $t$ is the time, in months, since the overfishing began." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186116434 1064 "In this lesson, we will learn how to interpret various aspects of an exponential decay expression or function.\n\nFor example, suppose that, due to overfishing, a fish population decays exponentially. The population $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = 5\\,000 \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in months, since the overfishing began. What can we deduce about the population of fish from this model?\n\nThe first thing to notice is that the function above is an exponential decay function of the form\n\n$$\ny(t) = a \\cdot b^t.\n$$\n\nSo, we can deduce the following:\n\n- $a=5\\,000$ represents the initial value. So this tells us that there were $5\\,000$ fish when the overfishing began.\n\n- $b = \\dfrac 1 2$ is the decay factor. Since $0 < b < 1,$ this tells us that we're dealing with exponential decay, *not* exponential growth (for exponential growth we need $b > 1$).\n\n- $t$ is the time, in months, since the overfishing began." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186121187 1078 #edb/ref 17592186121190 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186124587 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508918 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186124588 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508975 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186124589 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508990 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186124594 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508977 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186124651 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508993 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186124651 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508999 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186124652 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508945 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186124697 1050 420.0 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186124698 1050 420.0 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186124700 1050 540.0 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186124765 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186124765 1071 "Let $D$ be the set of all $2 \\cdot 2$ matrices of the form $\\begin{bmatrix}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{bmatrix}$, where $a ∈ R$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The identity matrix $I_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $D$.\n2. For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A, B ∈ D$, we have $A + O = O$.\n3. For any $A, B ∈ D$, we have $A + B = B + A$." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186124765 1071 "Let $D$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}],$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. The identity matrix ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $D.$\n\nII. For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A,B\\in D,$ we have $A+O=O.$\n\nIII. For any $A,B\\in D,$ we have $A+B=B+A.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186124765 1074 "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, every element $A$ in $D$ satisfies ${a}_{12}={a}_{21}=0$ and ${a}_{11}={a}_{22}.$ So, ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in D.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the identity matrix ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in D.$ Then\n\n$$\n{I}_{2}+O=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\ne O.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, For any matrices $A,B\\in D,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\nA+B=B+A\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is commutative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186124878 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121868 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186125238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186124028 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186125828 1064 "Consider the quadric surface\n\n$$\nz^2 = x^2 + y^2\n$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5a23dfa8bf6a0efec59763b9a2ec6d22d878f75babdbed9fc04dc8a7106f6ba.png)\n\nThis is an example of an **elliptic cone**. It has its **vertex** at the origin, and the $z$-axis is the **axis of symmetry** of the cone.\n\nMore generally, the equation of an elliptic cone whose vertex is at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(z-z_0)^2 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2},\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186125828 1064 "Consider the quadric surface\n\n$$z^2 = x^2 + y^2$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5a23dfa8bf6a0efec59763b9a2ec6d22d878f75babdbed9fc04dc8a7106f6ba.png)\n\nThis is an example of an **elliptic cone**. It has its **vertex** at the origin, and the $z$-axis is the **axis of symmetry** of the cone.\n\nMore generally, the equation of an elliptic cone whose vertex is at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$(z-z_0)^2 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2},$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186125830 1064 "A **cylinder** is a surface made by the set of lines that pass through a plane curve $C$ in the $xy$-plane and are perpendicular to this plane. The curve $C$ is called the **base curve**.\n\nThe familiar two-dimensional conic sections (circles, ellipses, parabolas, and hyperbolas) can be used to describe cylinders. In this lesson, we'll explore these shapes.\n\nAn **elliptic cylinder** is a cylinder whose base curve $C$ is an ellipse. If the ellipse lies in the $xy$-plane and is centered at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of our elliptic cylinder is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} = 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are the semi-axes of the ellipse $C.$ In particular, if $a=b$, we get a **circular cylinder.**\n\n**Watch out!** This may look like the equation of a regular ellipse. However, since we're now describing a surface in three-dimensional space, we assume that the $z$-coordinate is arbitrary. Thus, if we want $z$ to feature in our equations explicitly, we would write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} = 1, \\qquad z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in \\mathbb R.$ \n\nFor example, the graph of the elliptic cylinder\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + y^2 = 1\n$$\n\nis shown below. The center of its base ellipse $C$ is at the origin, and the **axis** of the elliptic cylinder (the line through the center of the cylinder) is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/50/504e609e134654d258378226d90f5faa8b9f6d12313a4fcac4a23d0bfe6bea52.png)\n\nLet's describe some properties of this elliptic cylinder:\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + y^2 = 1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the base ellipse $C$ centered at the origin $(0,0,0)$ with semi-major and semi-minor axes of lengths $2$ and $1,$ respectively.\n\n![](images/d0/d0e4c0630b0da60c4324330211ab1bf8090ebec067d9ebc2ac2a03056c500bd6.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{0}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to two lines in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](images/38/3813a5f43319056be03ddd8b3353ef3a8166b7495b32397c91066f40d0b33cd5.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to two lines in the $yz$-plane.\n\n![](images/a0/a0d928060ad18d3ba677f5dd184777b833d3abf03226c04c21292e455bcd0518.png)" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186125830 1064 "A **cylinder** is a surface made by the set of lines that pass through a plane curve $C$ in the $xy$-plane and are perpendicular to this plane. The curve $C$ is called the **base curve**.\n\nThe familiar two-dimensional conic sections (circles, ellipses, parabolas, and hyperbolas) can be used to describe cylinders. In this lesson, we'll explore these shapes.\n\nAn **elliptic cylinder** is a cylinder whose base curve $C$ is an ellipse. If the ellipse lies in the $xy$-plane and is centered at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of our elliptic cylinder is given by\n\n$$\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} = 1,$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are the semi-axes of the ellipse $C.$ In particular, if $a=b$, we get a **circular cylinder.**\n\n**Watch out!** This may look like the equation of a regular ellipse. However, since we're now describing a surface in three-dimensional space, we assume that the $z$-coordinate is arbitrary. Thus, if we want $z$ to feature in our equations explicitly, we would write\n\n$$\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} = 1, \\qquad z=k$$ where $k\\in \\mathbb R.$\n\nFor example, the graph of the elliptic cylinder\n\n$$\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + y^2 = 1$$\n\nis shown below. The center of its base ellipse $C$ is at the origin, and the **axis** of the elliptic cylinder (the line through the center of the cylinder) is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/50/504e609e134654d258378226d90f5faa8b9f6d12313a4fcac4a23d0bfe6bea52.png)\n\nLet's describe some properties of this elliptic cylinder:\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation $$\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + y^2 = 1,$$ which is the base ellipse $C$ centered at the origin $(0,0,0)$ with semi-major and semi-minor axes of lengths $2$ and $1,$ respectively.\n\n![](images/d0/d0e4c0630b0da60c4324330211ab1bf8090ebec067d9ebc2ac2a03056c500bd6.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{0}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to two lines in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](images/38/3813a5f43319056be03ddd8b3353ef3a8166b7495b32397c91066f40d0b33cd5.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1,\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to two lines in the $yz$-plane.\n\n![](images/a0/a0d928060ad18d3ba677f5dd184777b833d3abf03226c04c21292e455bcd0518.png)" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186125831 1064 "A **parabolic cylinder** is a cylinder whose base curve $C$ is a parabola.\n\nIf the parabola $C$ opens upward or downward when viewed from the $xy$-plane and its vertex is at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of the parabolic cylinder is given by\n\n$$\n(x-x_0)^2 = 4p(y-y_0),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nFor example, the graph of the parabolic cylinder\n\n$$\nx^2 = y\n$$\n\nis shown below. Its base curve is an upward-opening parabola in the $xy$-plane with its vertex at the origin. The **axis** of the cylinder is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/03/03ab0a28802900d8076fdd5f555bdad6582c449441c46f1de74260013cc55119.png)\n\nThe trace of this parabolic cylinder can be either a parabola or a set of lines.\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\nx^2 = y,\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to the base parabola $C$ with its vertex at the origin $(0,0,0).$\n\n![](images/98/98124890d921c73d0cda54428ac803221d5f393384e14db2909b0138fda0fb93.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to a line in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](images/f8/f87dbd180911db28a515dfe11da99d5d9d06da74e7ef117ea0a6df88018e95a2.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2} & =y \\\\ y & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to a line in the $yz$-plane.\n\n![](images/f8/f87dbd180911db28a515dfe11da99d5d9d06da74e7ef117ea0a6df88018e95a2.png)\n\nOn the other hand, if the base parabola $C$ is left-opening or right-opening when viewed from the $xy$-plane and its vertex is at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of the parabolic cylinder is given by\n\n$$\n(y-y_0)^2 = 4p(x-x_0),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nFor example, the graph of the parabolic cylinder\n\n$$\ny^2 = x\n$$\n\nis shown below. Its base curve is a right-opening parabola in the $xy$-plane with its vertex at the origin. The cylinder's axis is the $z$-axis.\n\n  \n\n![](images/a8/a87267dbd77b1561c047c6228ba0c70b08e5a48228d7e0b6a161463045d5e22b.png)" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186125831 1064 "A **parabolic cylinder** is a cylinder whose base curve $C$ is a parabola.\n\nIf the parabola $C$ opens upward or downward when viewed from the $xy$-plane and its vertex is at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of the parabolic cylinder is given by $$(x-x_0)^2 = 4p(y-y_0),$$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\nFor example, the graph of the parabolic cylinder $$x^2 = y$$ is shown below. Its base curve is an upward-opening parabola in the $xy$-plane with its vertex at the origin. The **axis** of the cylinder is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/03/03ab0a28802900d8076fdd5f555bdad6582c449441c46f1de74260013cc55119.png)\n\nThe trace of this parabolic cylinder can be either a parabola or a set of lines.\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation $$x^2 = y,$$ which corresponds to the base parabola $C$ with its vertex at the origin $(0,0,0).$\n\n![](images/98/98124890d921c73d0cda54428ac803221d5f393384e14db2909b0138fda0fb93.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to a line in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](images/f8/f87dbd180911db28a515dfe11da99d5d9d06da74e7ef117ea0a6df88018e95a2.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2} & =y \\\\ y & =0,\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to a line in the $yz$-plane.\n\n![](images/f8/f87dbd180911db28a515dfe11da99d5d9d06da74e7ef117ea0a6df88018e95a2.png)\n\nOn the other hand, if the base parabola $C$ is left-opening or right-opening when viewed from the $xy$-plane and its vertex is at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of the parabolic cylinder is given by $$(y-y_0)^2 = 4p(x-x_0),$$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\nFor example, the graph of the parabolic cylinder $$y^2 = x$$ is shown below. Its base curve is a right-opening parabola in the $xy$-plane with its vertex at the origin. The cylinder's axis is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/a8/a87267dbd77b1561c047c6228ba0c70b08e5a48228d7e0b6a161463045d5e22b.png)" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186125834 1064 "A **hyperbolic cylinder** is a cylinder whose base curve $C$ is a hyperbola.\n\nIf the hyperbola $C$ is horizontal in the $xy$-plane and is centered at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of the hyperbolic cylinder is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} = 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\n For example, the graph of the hyperbola cylinder\n\n$$\nx^2 - y^2 = 1\n$$\n\nis shown below. Its base curve is a horizontal hyperbola in the $xy$-plane centered at the origin. The **axis** of the cylinder is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/e3/e3c921cc7d6a1edbb182ecf158d7b84eb0d203fd20924d07cf57ca8f8ffdc107.png)\n\nThe trace of this surface can be either a hyperbola, a set of lines, or the empty set.\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\nx^2 - y^2 = 1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the base hyperbola $C$ centered at the origin $(0,0,0).$\n\n![](images/19/19e02f27f625dd75d12333de3b82a7a012e4f6370c729eef5cd38a633c2c8689.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{0}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to a pair of lines in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](images/56/5679b53433493d8b6e92d5aa5273be30bbf67a9db51993a925423d8514571f05.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is the empty set.\n\nOn the other hand, if the base hyperbola $C$ is vertical when viewed from the $xy$-plane and is centered at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of the hyperbolic cylinder is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} - \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} = 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nFor example, the graph of the hyperbola cylinder\n\n$$\ny^2 - x^2 = 1\n$$\n\nis shown below. Its base curve is a vertical hyperbola in the $xy$-plane centered at the origin, and the cylinder's axis is the $z$-axis.\n\n  \n\n![](images/d6/d690bca9515389bb403d0f205861af3294bc3a961df6691a5adb86de01421cff.png)\n\nNote that for all of the cylinders discussed so far, the *domain* of each cylinder in the $xy$-plane is simply the base curve $C.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186125834 1064 "A **hyperbolic cylinder** is a cylinder whose base curve $C$ is a hyperbola.\n\nIf the hyperbola $C$ is horizontal in the $xy$-plane and is centered at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of the hyperbolic cylinder is given by $$\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} = 1,$$ where $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nFor example, the graph of the hyperbola cylinder $$x^2 - y^2 = 1$$ is shown below. Its base curve is a horizontal hyperbola in the $xy$-plane centered at the origin. The **axis** of the cylinder is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/e3/e3c921cc7d6a1edbb182ecf158d7b84eb0d203fd20924d07cf57ca8f8ffdc107.png)\n\nThe trace of this surface can be either a hyperbola, a set of lines, or the empty set.\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation $$x^2 - y^2 = 1,$$ which is the base hyperbola $C$ centered at the origin $(0,0,0).$\n\n![](images/19/19e02f27f625dd75d12333de3b82a7a012e4f6370c729eef5cd38a633c2c8689.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{0}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1,\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to a pair of lines in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](images/56/5679b53433493d8b6e92d5aa5273be30bbf67a9db51993a925423d8514571f05.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is the empty set.\n\nOn the other hand, if the base hyperbola $C$ is vertical when viewed from the $xy$-plane and is centered at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of the hyperbolic cylinder is given by $$\\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} - \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} = 1,$$ where $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nFor example, the graph of the hyperbola cylinder $$y^2 - x^2 = 1$$ is shown below. Its base curve is a vertical hyperbola in the $xy$-plane centered at the origin, and the cylinder's axis is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/d6/d690bca9515389bb403d0f205861af3294bc3a961df6691a5adb86de01421cff.png)\n\nNote that for all of the cylinders discussed so far, the *domain* of each cylinder in the $xy$-plane is simply the base curve $C.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [