th its axis of symmetry along the $y$-axis is given by $$\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} = 1.$$\n- We can shift the center/vertex of the base curve in the usual way." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186125888 1064 "The most common quadric surfaces are those centered at the origin. We describe these common surfaces in the table below.\n\n| Surface | Equation | Image |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Ellipsoid | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}+\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} = 1$ | ![](images/09/09dc100248a28a5518bcd8428368edefc7c3ee8d5ef0e50515cb7552ee54a376.png) |\n| Elliptic Paraboloid | $z = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}$ | ![](images/76/76e4ad8cb1e83eadae4dce865e100c9a716f9830e7f12adb367b2a9b74d1a651.png) |\n| Hyperbolic Paraboloid | $z = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}-\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}$ | ![](images/cb/cbc04d7f98e1bb7ce0d62eda71102a6abad708dfcedd7aabee8fff23281471dd.png) |\n| Hyperboloid of One Sheet | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}-\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=1$ | ![](images/df/df6ef914bc78e121d75baf9f5147784d0e6e434d76800eff60dcb3c3d764477c.png) |\n| Hyperboloid of Two Sheets | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}-\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=-1$ | ![](images/08/0870b95c5ce1133d6645e3063174157e36c4354df370c05e48218111ea714838.png) |\n| Elliptic Cone | $\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}$ | ![](images/0f/0f492bf07e573ec7858977e48fa4bf81635e61520b5caa9edef14bd259184347.png) |\n\nAlso, recall the following from previous lessons:\n\n- We can generate paraboloids, hyperboloids, and cones with alternative orientation axes by permuting the variables in the corresponding equation.\n\nFor example, the equation of an elliptic cone with its vertex at $O$ and axis of symmetry along the $y$-axis is given by $$\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}.$$\n- We can shift the center/vertex/saddle point of a quadric surface to $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ using the usual coordinate transformation: $$(x,y,z) \\longrightarrow (x-x_0,y-y_0,z-z_0)$$\n\nFinally, there is a simple way to distinguish between hyperboloids with one and two sheets by simply looking at the equations:\n\n- *one* minus sign indicates hyperboloids of *one* sheet: $$\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=1$$\n- *two* minus signs indicate hyperboloids of *two* sheets: $$\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-}1$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186125888 1064 "The most common quadric surfaces are those centered at the origin. We describe these common surfaces in the table below.\n\n| Surface | Equation | Image |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Ellipsoid | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}+\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} = 1$ | ![](images/09/09dc100248a28a5518bcd8428368edefc7c3ee8d5ef0e50515cb7552ee54a376.png) |\n| Elliptic Paraboloid | $z = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}$ | ![](images/76/76e4ad8cb1e83eadae4dce865e100c9a716f9830e7f12adb367b2a9b74d1a651.png) |\n| Hyperbolic Paraboloid | $z = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}-\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}$ | ![](images/cb/cbc04d7f98e1bb7ce0d62eda71102a6abad708dfcedd7aabee8fff23281471dd.png) |\n| Hyperboloid of One Sheet | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}-\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=1$ | ![](images/df/df6ef914bc78e121d75baf9f5147784d0e6e434d76800eff60dcb3c3d764477c.png) |\n| Hyperboloid of Two Sheets | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}-\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=-1$ | ![](images/08/0870b95c5ce1133d6645e3063174157e36c4354df370c05e48218111ea714838.png) |\n| Elliptic Cone | $\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}$ | ![](images/0f/0f492bf07e573ec7858977e48fa4bf81635e61520b5caa9edef14bd259184347.png) |\n\nAlso, recall the following from previous lessons:\n\n- We can generate paraboloids, hyperboloids, and cones with alternative orientation axes by permuting the variables in the corresponding equation. \n\nFor example, the equation of an elliptic cone with its vertex at $O$ and axis of symmetry along the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}.\n$$\n\n- We can shift the center/vertex/saddle point of a quadric surface to $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ using the usual coordinate transformation:\n\n$$\n(x,y,z) \\longrightarrow (x-x_0,y-y_0,z-z_0)\n$$\n\nFinally, there is a simple way to distinguish between hyperboloids with one and two sheets by simply looking at the equations:\n\n- *one* minus sign indicates hyperboloids of *one* sheet:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=1\n$$\n\n- *two* minus signs indicate hyperboloids of *two* sheets:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-}1\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186127117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127118 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186127117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127119 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186127117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127120 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186127117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127121 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186127117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127122 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186127117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508399 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186127117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508441 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186127117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508594 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186127117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508700 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186127117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508763 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186127117 1078 #edb/ref 17592186508700 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186129161 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508957 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186129326 1050 420.0 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186129606 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186129606 1071 "For the function $f(x, y, z) = \\ln (\\sqrt{yz}) - \\sqrt{x + 1}$, find the equation of the level surface $f(x, y, z) = 0$." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186129606 1071 "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{yz})-\\sqrt{x+1},$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186129606 1074 "Recall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=0.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{yz})-\\sqrt{x+1} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{yz}) & =\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\ \\sqrt{yz} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{x+1}} \\\\ yz & ={({e}^{\\sqrt{x+1}})}^{2} \\\\ z & =\\frac{{e}^{2\\sqrt{x+1}}}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=0$ is the surface $z=\\frac{{e}^{2\\sqrt{x+1}}}{y}.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186147207 1071 "The number of members $m$ of a club after $t$ years is given by the formula\n\n$m(t) = 766 \\cdot (\\textrm{___})^{t}$.\n\nIf there were $766$ members initially, and the number of members decreases by $22\\%$ each year, what is the missing value in the expression for $m(t)$?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186147207 1071 "The number of members $m$ of a club after $t$ years is given by the formula \n\n$$\nm(t) = 766 \\cdot (\\textrm{___})^{t}.\n$$\n\nIf there were $766$ members initially, and the number of members decreases by $22\\%$ each year, what is the missing value in the expression for $m(t)?$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186147207 1074 "The formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nm(t) = a(1-r)^t\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=766$ is the initial number of members.\n- $r = 0.22$ is the decay rate ($22\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $m$ is\n\n$$\nm(t)= 766\\cdot (1 - 0.22)^{t} = 766\\cdot (0.78)^{t}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $0.78$." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186147207 1074 "The formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nm(t) = a(1-r)^t.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=766$ is the initial number of members.\n\n- $r = 0.22$ is the decay rate ($22\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $m$ is\n\n$$\nm(t)= 766\\cdot (1 - 0.22)^{t} = 766\\cdot (0.78)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $0.78.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186147208 1071 "A mountain has a glacier with a depth of $75\\,\\textrm{m}$. The depth of the glacier reduces by $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ every year. Construct a function that represents the depth $d(t)$ of the glacier after $t$ years." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186147208 1071 "A mountain has a glacier with a depth of $75\\,\\textrm{m}.$ The depth of the glacier reduces by $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ every year. Construct a function that represents the depth $d(t)$ of the glacier after $t$ years." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186147208 1074 "The depth of the glacier is $75\\,\\textrm{m}$, and this reduces by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nevery year. Therefore, the depth decays exponentially with a decay factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 4\n$$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay factor $b$) is\n\n$$\nd(t) = ab^t\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=75 \\textrm{m}$ is the initial depth of the glacier.\n- $b = \\dfrac 3 4$ is the decay factor.\n- $t =$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for the depth $d(t)$ is\n\n$$\nd(t)= 75 \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac 3 4\\right)^{t}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186147208 1074 "The depth of the glacier is $75\\,\\textrm{m},$ and this reduces by $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ every year. Therefore, the depth decays exponentially with a decay factor of $\\dfrac 3 4.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay factor $b$) is\n\n$$\nd(t) = ab^t.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=75 \\textrm{ m }$ is the initial depth of the glacier.\n\n- $b = \\dfrac 3 4$ is the decay factor.\n\n- $t =$  the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for the depth $d(t)$ is\n\n$$\nd(t)= 75 \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac 3 4\\right)^{t}.\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186147570 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508933 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186147570 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508944 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186147571 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508949 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186147571 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508994 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186147672 1050 420.0 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186148687 1064 "To model an exponential decay situation, it is often useful to start by setting up a function which represents the exponential decay. Then, we can evaluate that function as needed.\n\nFor example, suppose that a car's value depreciates by one-fifth every year. If the car was purchased for $\\[math]25\\,000$, then what function gives the car's value after $t$ years?\n\nFirst, note that if the car's value reduces by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 5\n$$\n\nevery year, then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 4 5\n$$\n\nof its value remains every year.\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 25\\,000$ is the **initial value** of the car (in dollars).\n- $b = \\dfrac 4 5$ is the **decay factor**.\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nLet $y(t)$ be the car's value after $t$ years. The formula for $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = ab^t\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\ny(t) = 25\\,000\\left(\\dfrac 4 5\\right)^t\n$$\n\nTo find the value of the car after $5$ years, we substitute $t=5$ into the above:\n\n$$\ny(5) = 25000 \\cdot (\\frac{4}{5})^{5} = 8192\n$$\n\nTherefore, the car will be worth $\\[/math] 8\\,192$ in $5$ years." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186148687 1064 "To model an exponential decay situation, it is often useful to start by setting up a function which represents the exponential decay. Then, we can evaluate that function as needed.\n\nFor example, suppose that a car's value depreciates by one-fifth every year. If the car was purchased for $\\[math]25\\,000,$ then what function gives the car's value after $t$ years? \n\nFirst, note that if the car's value reduces by $\\dfrac 1 5$ every year, then $\\dfrac 4 5$ of its value remains every year.\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 25\\,000$ is the **initial value** of the car (in dollars).\n\n- $b = \\dfrac 4 5$ is the **decay factor**.\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nLet $y(t)$ be the car's value after $t$ years. The formula for $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = ab^t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $a$ and $b,$ we get\n\n$$\ny(t) = 25\\,000\\left(\\dfrac 4 5\\right)^t.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of the car after $5$ years, we substitute $t=5$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(5)=25\\,000\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{5})}^{5}=8\\,192\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the car will be worth $\\[/math] 8\\,192$ in $5$ years." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186148688 1064 "It's common to use a **decay rate** (rather than a decay factor) when describing exponential decay. A decay rate is typically expressed using a percent.\n\nFor example, suppose that after treatment with a particular antibiotic, the number of infected cells in a specimen decreased by $30\\%$ per hour. If there were approximately $5$ million infected cells at the beginning of the treatment, how many infected cells remained after $3$ hours?\n\nLet $y(t)$ be the number of infected cells after $t$ hours. The formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny = a(1-r)^t\n$$\n\nNotice that this is very similar to the formula for exponential growth. The difference here is that we have a minus $(-)$ instead of a plus $(+)$ in the parentheses.\n\nIn our case, we have:\n\n- $a=5$ is the initial number of infected cells (in millions).\n- $r = 0.30$ is the decay rate ($30\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n- $t=$ the time, in hours.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny = 5(1-0.3)^{t}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above gives\n\n$$\ny(t) =5(0.7)^{t}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the function for $t=3$ hours, we get\n\n$$\ny(3) = 5(0.7)^{3} = 1.715\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $3$ hours, there were $1.715$ million infected cells.\n\nWhen writing down a formula for exponential decay, the key takeaway is this:\n\n- If we're given a **decay rate** (usually expressed as a percent), then we use the formula $y(t) = a(1-r)^t$.\n- If we're given a **decay factor**, then we use the formula $y(t) = ab^t$." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186148688 1064 "It's common to use a **decay rate** (rather than a decay factor) when describing exponential decay. A decay rate is typically expressed using a percent.\n\nFor example, suppose that after treatment with a particular antibiotic, the number of infected cells in a specimen decreased by $30\\%$ per hour. If there were approximately $5$ million infected cells at the beginning of the treatment, how many infected cells remained after $3$ hours?\n\nLet $y(t)$ be the number of infected cells after $t$ hours. The formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny = a(1-r)^t.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is very similar to the formula for exponential growth. The difference here is that we have a minus $(-)$ instead of a plus $(+)$ in the parentheses.\n\nIn our case, we have:\n\n- $a=5$ is the initial number of infected cells (in millions).\n\n- $r = 0.30$ is the decay rate ($30\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in hours.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny  = 5(1-0.3)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above gives\n\n$$\ny(t) =5(0.7)^{t}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the function for $t=3$ hours, we get\n\n$$\ny(3) = 5(0.7)^{3} = 1.715.\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $3$ hours, there were $1.715$ million infected cells.\n\nWhen writing down a formula for exponential decay, the key takeaway is this:\n\n- If we're given a **decay rate** (usually expressed as a percent), then we use the formula $y(t) = a(1-r)^t.$\n\n- If we're given a **decay factor**, then we use the formula $y(t) = ab^t.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186157456 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508931 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186157457 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508936 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186157457 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508968 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186166088 1078 #edb/ref 17592186166092 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186166094 1078 #edb/ref 17592186166096 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186166887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508987 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186166888 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508995 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186166889 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508997 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186167480 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186167480 1071 "Given $X∼B(7, 0.6)$, compute $P(1 < X \\le 3)$. Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186167480 1071 "Given $X∼B(7,0.6),$ compute $P(1<X\\le 3).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186167480 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,0.6),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.6)^{x}(0.4)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{2})(0.6)^{2}(0.4)^{7-2}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{3})(0.6)^{3}(0.4)^{7-3} \\\\  & =21(0.6)^{2}(0.4)^{5}+35(0.6)^{3}(0.4)^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.2710\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186167481 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186167481 1071 "Given that $X∼B(9, 0.3)$, compute $P(X \\le 7)$. Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186167481 1071 "Given that $X∼B(9,0.3),$ compute $P(X\\le 7).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186167481 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(9,0.3),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{x})(0.3)^{x}(0.7)^{9-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X\\le 7)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,…,7.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X>7)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=8,9,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X>7),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>7) & =P(X\\in \\{8,9\\}) \\\\  & =f(8)+f(9) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{8})(0.3)^{8}(0.7)^{9-8}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{9})(0.3)^{9}(0.7)^{9-9} \\\\  & =9(0.3)^{8}(0.7)+(0.3)^{9} \\\\  & ≈0.0004\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 7) & =1-P(X>7) \\\\  & ≈1-0.0004 \\\\  & =0.9996\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186175901 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508954 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186176019 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508953 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186176019 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508979 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186176022 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508911 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186176022 1038