=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & -12 \\\\ 0 & 16 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 0 & 16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 0 & -12 \\\\ 0 & 16-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -12 & 0 & 16-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (16-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (16-\\lambda )((9-\\lambda )(16-\\lambda )-144) & =0 \\\\ (16-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda -16\\lambda +144-144) & =0 \\\\ (16-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-25\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -16)(\\lambda -25) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =25,\\,16,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{25}=5 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{16}=4 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186191187 1075 #edb/ref 17592186191188 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186191187 1075 #edb/ref 17592186191190 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186191187 1075 #edb/ref 17592186191191 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186191187 1075 #edb/ref 17592186191192 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186191187 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508564 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186191187 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508587 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186191187 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508651 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186191187 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508791 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186191187 1078 #edb/ref 17592186191189 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186191245 1078 #edb/ref 17592186191246 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186191248 1078 #edb/ref 17592186191250 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186191552 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508529 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186191552 1078 #edb/ref 17592186508529 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186191553 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508699 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186191553 1078 #edb/ref 17592186508699 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186192585 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n${a + 4b = 1; 2a + b =-5$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $a$ from this system by filling in the missing information below.\n\n$\\qquad$ *To eliminate $a$, multiply the $\\,\\textbf{__________}\\,$equation by $\\,\\textbf{______}\\,$,\nand add the equations.*" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186192585 1071 "Consider the following system of equations. \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}a+4b=1 \\\\ 2a+b=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $a$ from this system by filling in the missing information below.\n\n$\\qquad$ *To eliminate $a,$ multiply the $\\,\\textbf{__________}\\,$equation by $\\,\\textbf{______}\\,$, \nand add the equations.*" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186192585 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $a$ in the first equation is $1$, while in the second equation it is $2$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}-2}$, then the coefficient of $a$ will turn into $-2$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n{(a + 4b = 1)\\cdot (-2); 2a + b =-5\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $a$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2a - 8b &= -2 \\\\\n2a + b &= -5 \\\\\n(-8b + b) &= (-2 - 5) \\\\\n- 7b &= -7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $a$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-2}$, and add the equations*." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186192585 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $a$ in the first equation is $1,$ while in the second equation it is $2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}-2},$ then the coefficient of $a$ will turn into $-2,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the second equation.\n\n![](images/2b/2b0aa0d1dbfeb1e711bde6e7741fe5706aa126d71c44ae417d3c78d01b83547a.svg)\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $a$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{-2a}-8b & =-2 \\\\ \\cancel{2a}+b & =-5 \\\\ (-8b+b) & =(-2-5) \\\\ -7b & =-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $a,$ multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{first}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-2},$ and add the equations*." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186192586 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y=2 \\\\ x-2y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $y$ from this system by filling in the missing information below.\n\n$\\qquad$ *To eliminate $y,$ multiply the $\\,\\textbf{__________}\\,$equation by $\\,\\textbf{______}\\,$, \nand add the equations.*" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186192586 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n${3x + 4y = 2; x - 2y = 4$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $y$ from this system by filling in the missing information below.\n\n$\\qquad$ *To eliminate $y$, multiply the $\\,\\textbf{__________}\\,$equation by $\\,\\textbf{______}\\,$,\nand add the equations.*" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186192586 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $4$, while in the second equation it is $-2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by ${\\color{blue}2}$, then the coefficient of $y$ will turn into $-4$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n{(3x + 4y = 2; (x - 2y = 4)\\cdot (2)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + 4y &= 2 \\\\\n2x - 4y &= 8 \\\\\n(3x + 2x) &= (2 + 8) \\\\\n5x &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $y$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$, and add the equations*." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186192586 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $4,$ while in the second equation it is $-2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by ${\\color{blue}2},$ then the coefficient of $y$ will turn into $-4,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the first equation.\n\n![](images/0e/0e494f68d4859d46c3c369274e415f10a850c664343d41cce8dcfcfdd660e17c.svg)\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+\\cancel{4y} & =2 \\\\ 2x-\\cancel{4y} & =8 \\\\ (3x+2x) & =(2+8) \\\\ 5x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $y,$ multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2},$ and add the equations*." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186192587 1071 "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y=8 \\\\ x-2y=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186192587 1071 "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n${2x + 3y = 8; x - 2y =-3$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186192587 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $2$, while in the second equation it is $1$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-2$, then the $x$-coefficient will become $-2$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n{(2x + 3y = 8; (x - 2y =-3) \\cdot (-2)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 3y &= 8 \\\\\n- 2x + 4y &= 6 \\\\\n(3y + 4y) &= (8 + 6) \\\\\n7y &= 14 \\\\\ny &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y = 2$. However, we still need to find $x$. To do this, we substitute $y = 2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2y &= -3 \\\\\nx - 2(2) &= -3 \\\\\nx - 4 &= -3 \\\\\nx &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=1, \\,y=2$, which can be expressed as $(1,2)$." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186192587 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $2,$ while in the second equation it is $1.$\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-2,$ then the $x$-coefficient will become $-2,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the first equation.\n![](images/a7/a7e5f9f09d67dd14a4797e5675585973e3aafeb1ee96e9021d245f49ce9c1c7e.svg)\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{2x}+3y & =8 \\\\ \\cancel{-2x}+4y & =6 \\\\ (3y+4y) & =(8+6) \\\\ 7y & =14 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y = 2.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y = 2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x-2y & =-3 \\\\ x-2(2) & =-3 \\\\ x-4 & =-3 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=1, \\,y=2,$ which can be expressed as $(1,2).$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186192588 1071 "Find the solution $(u,v)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5u-6v=3 \\\\ -3u+2v=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186192588 1071 "Find the solution $(u,v)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n${5u - 6v = 3; - 3u + 2v =-5$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186192588 1074 "Notice that the $v$-coefficient in the first equation is $-6$, while in the second equation it is $2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $3$, then the $v$-coefficient will become $6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-6$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n{5u - 6v = 3; (-3u + 2v =-5) \\cdot (3)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $v$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5u - 6v &= 3 \\\\\n- 9u + 6v &= -15 \\\\\n(5u - 9u) &= (3 - 15) \\\\\n- 4u &= -12 \\\\\nu &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $u = 3$. However, we still need to find $v$. To do this, we substitute $u = 3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3u + 2v &= -5 \\\\\n- 3(3) + 2v &= -5 \\\\\n- 9 + 2v &= -5 \\\\\n2v &= 4 \\\\\nv &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nu = 3, \\,v = 2\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(3,2)$." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186192588 1074 "Notice that the $v$-coefficient in the first equation is $-6,$ while in the second equation it is $2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $3,$ then the $v$-coefficient will become $6,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $-6$ in the first equation.\n![](images/e7/e701d299976539abdbbe6e9c311c2db14c23cca6817c5a3de5b5c05a95760d99.svg)\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $v$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}5u-\\cancel{6v} & =3 \\\\ -9u+\\cancel{6v} & =-15 \\\\ (5u-9u) & =(3-15) \\\\ -4u & =-12 \\\\ u & =3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $u = 3.$ However, we still need to find $v.$ To do this, we substitute $u = 3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3u+2v & =-5 \\\\ -3(3)+2v & =-5 \\\\ -9+2v & =-5 \\\\ 2v & =4 \\\\ v & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u = 3, \\,v = 2,$ which can be expressed as $(3,2).$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186192654 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508917 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186192655 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508913 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186192655 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508932 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186192656 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508926 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186192657 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508927 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186192905 1050 660.0 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186193226 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186193226 1071 "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n${4x - 5y = 0; 2x - 3y = 2$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $x$.*" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186193226 1071 "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-5y=0 \\\\ 2x-3y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $x.$*" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186193226 1074 "Notice that the $x$-coefficient in the first equation is $4,$ while in the second equation it is $2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-2,$ then the $x$-coefficient will become $-4,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-5y=0 \\\\ (\\,2x-3y=2\\,)\\,\\times (-2)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-5y=0 \\\\ -4x+6y=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{4x}-5y & =0 \\\\ -\\cancel{4x}+6y & =-4 \\\\ (-5y+6y) & =(0-4) \\\\ y & =-4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=-4.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=-4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}4x-5y & =0 \\\\ 4x-5(-4) & =0 \\\\ 4x+20 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =-20 \\\\ x & =-5\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-5,\\,y=-4,$ which can be expressed as $(-5,-4).$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186193242 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186193242 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n${3s + 4t = 8; s + 5t = 10$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $s$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $s$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186193242 1071 "Consider the following system of equations. \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3s+4t=8 \\\\ s+5t=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $s$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $s,$ multiply the \n{{field-1}} \nequation by \n{{field-2}}, \nand add the equations." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186193242 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $s$ in the first equation is $3,$ while in the second equation it is $1.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-3,$ then the coefficient of $s$ will turn into $-3,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $3$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3s+4t=8 \\\\ (\\,s+5t=10\\,)\\,\\times (-3)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3s+4t=8 \\\\ -3s-15t=-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $s$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{3s}+4t & =8 \\\\ -\\cancel{3s}-15t & =-30 \\\\ (4t-15t) & =(8-30) \\\\ -11t & =-22 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*Multiply the $\\text{second}$ equation by $-3,$ and add the equations*." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186193285 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186193285 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x+2y=11 \\\\ 7x+4y=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x,y)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $y.$\n\nThe solution is $($\n{{field-1}}$\\,,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186193285 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n${-5x + 2y = 11; 7x + 4y = 5$\n\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $y$.\n\nThe solution is $($\n{{field-1}} $$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186193285 1074 "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $2,$ while in the second equation it is $4.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $-2,$ then the $y$-coefficient will become $-4,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,-5x+2y=11\\,)\\,\\times (-2) \\\\ 7x+4y=5\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}10x-4y=-22 \\\\ 7x+4y=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x-\\cancel{4y} & =-22 \\\\ 7x+\\cancel{4y} & =5 \\\\ (10x+7x) & =(-22+5) \\\\ 17x & =-17 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=-1.$ However, we still need to find $y.$ To do this, we substitute $x=-1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x+4y & =5 \\\\ 7(-1)+4y & =5 \\\\ -7+4y & =5 \\\\ 4y & =12 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-1,\\,y=3,$ which can be expressed as $(-1,3).$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186193941 1064 "Suppose we want to solve the following system of linear equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=1 \\\\ x-y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs it stands, adding the two equations will not eliminate any of the variables. \n\nHowever, suppose we want to eliminate the variable $x$. We begin by multiplying the second equation by $\\color{blue}-1$, which gives the following equivalent system:\n\n![](images/72/724119f2404af61332fcabbb5cda3522f9d338a4c6427084e5901e1ca7cd4498.svg)\n\nThen, we add the two resulting equations together. This will eliminate the variable $x,$ allowing us to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{x}+2y & =1 \\\\ -\\cancel{x}+y & =2 \\\\ (2y+y) & =(1+2) \\\\ 3y & =3 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=1.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x-y & =-2 \\\\ x-(1) & =-2 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-1$ and $y=1,$ which can be expressed as $(-1, 1).$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186193941 1064 "Suppose we want to solve the following system of linear equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n{x + 2y = 1; x - y =-2\n$$\n\nAs it stands, adding the two equations will not eliminate any of the variables.\n\nHowever, suppose we want to eliminate the variable $x$. We begin by multiplying the second equation by $\\color{blue}-1$, which gives the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n{(x + 2y = 1; (x - y =-2)\\cdot (-1)\n$$\n\nThen, we add the two resulting equations together. This will eliminate the variable $x$, allowing us to solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 2y &= 1 \\\\\n- x + y &= 2 \\\\\n(2y + y) &= (1 + 2) \\\\\n3y &= 3 \\\\\ny &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=1$. However, we still need to find $x$. To do this, we substitute $y=1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - y &= -2 \\\\\nx - (1) &= -2 \\\\\nx &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-1$ and $y=1$, which can be expressed as $(-1, 1)$." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186227913 1078 #edb/ref 17592186227918 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186227998 1078 #edb/ref 17592186228000 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186228878 1071 "A discount of $14\\%$ is offered on all hiking boots. What is the sale price of a pair of boots originally priced at $\\[math]78$?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186228878 1071 "A discount of $14\\%$ is offered on all hiking boots. What is the sale price of a pair of boots originally priced at $\\[math]78?$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186228878 1074 "To find an amount that's $14\\%$ *less than* $\\[math]78$, we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% - 14\\% = 86\\%\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n86\\% = \\dfrac{86}{100} = 0.86\n$$\n\nSo, to find $86\\%$ of $\\[/math]78$, we multiply $\\[math]78$ by $0.86{:}$\n\n$$\n0.86 \\cdot \\[/math]78 = \\[math]67.08\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sale price of the hiking boots is $\\[/math]67.08$." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186228878 1074 "To find an amount that's $14\\%$ *less than* $\\[math]78,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% - 14\\% = 86\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n86\\% = \\dfrac{86}{100} = 0.86\n$$\n\nSo, to find $86\\%$ of $\\[/math]78,$ we multiply $\\[math]78$ by $0.86{:}$\n\n$$\n0.86 \\cdot \\[/math]78 = \\[math]67.08\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sale price of the hiking boots is $\\[/math]67.08.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186228888 1074 "To find a number $30\\%$ *less than* $120$, we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% - 30\\% = 70\\%\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n70\\% = \\dfrac{70}{100} = 0.7\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $70\\%$ of $120$, we multiply $120$ by $0.7{:}$\n\n$$\n0.7 \\cdot 120 = 84\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186228888 1074 "To find a number $30\\%$ *less than* $120,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% - 30\\% = 70\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n70\\% = \\dfrac{70}{100} = 0.7\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $70\\%$ of $120,$ we multiply $120$ by $0.7{:}$\n\n$$\n0.7 \\cdot 120 = 84\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186229115 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508992 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186229116 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508983 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186229244 1050 420.0 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186229595 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186229595 1071 "At a furniture store, all tables are discounted by $25%$ during a weekend sale. What is the sale price of a table that originally costs $$64$?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186229595 1071 "At a furniture store, all tables are discounted by $25%$ during a weekend sale. What is the sale price of a table that originally costs $$64?$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186229595 1074 "To find an amount that's $25%$ *less than* $$64,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%-25%=75%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n75%=\\frac{75}{100}=0.75\n$$\n\nSo, to find $75%$ of $$64,$ we multiply $$64$ by $0.75:$\n\n$$\n0.75\\cdot $64=$48\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sale price of the table is $$48.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186229610 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186229610 1071 "What number is $15%$ less than $80$?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186229610 1071 "What number is $15%$ less than $80?$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186229610 1074 "To find a number $15%$ *less than* $80,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%-15%=85%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n85%=\\frac{85}{100}=0.85\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $85%$ of $80,$ we multiply $80$ by $0.85:$\n\n$$\n0.85\\cdot 80=68\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186230172 1064 "In a previous lesson, we spent some time learning how to find a number that is a certain percentage *greater than* a given amount. This is known as finding a *percentage increase.*\n\nWe can apply similar reasoning to find a number that is a certain percentage *less than* a given amount. This corresponds to applying a **percentage decrease.**\n\nTo apply a percentage decrease, we do the following:\n\n1. Subtract the decrease from $100\\%$.\n2. Convert the result to a decimal multiplier.\n3. Find the product of the multiplier and the original number.\n\nLet's take a look at a worked example." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186230172 1064 "In a previous lesson, we spent some time learning how to find a number that is a certain percentage *greater than* a given amount. This is known as finding a *percentage increase.*\n\nWe can apply similar reasoning to find a number that is a certain percentage *less than* a given amount. This corresponds to applying a **percentage decrease.**\n\nTo apply a percentage decrease, we do the following:\n\n1\\. Subtract the decrease from $100\\%.$\n\n2\\. Convert the result to a decimal multiplier.\n\n3\\. Find the product of the multiplier and the original number.\n\nLet's take a look at a worked example." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186230242 1064 "Consider the following problem.\n\n> What number is $20\\%$ less than $250$?\n\nThe key words here are \"$20\\%$ *less than.*\" This means we need to apply a percentage decrease, decreasing the original number by $20\\%$.\n\nTo solve this problem, we follow three steps.\n\n- **Step 1.** Subtract the decrease from $100\\%$.To find a number $20\\%$ *less than* $250$, we apply a new percentage of\n$100\\% - 20\\% = 80\\%$.\nThis means that our new number is $80\\%$ of $250$.\n\n- **Step 2.** Convert the result to a decimal multiplier.Now, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n$80\\% = \\dfrac{80}{100} = 0.8$\n- **Step 3.** Find the product of the multiplier and the original number.So, to find a number that is $20\\%$ less than $250$, we multiply $250$ by $0.8{:}$\n$0.8 \\cdot 250 = 200$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $20\\%$ less than $250$ is $200$." 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186230242 1064 "Consider the following problem.\n\nWhat number is $20\\%$ less than $250?$\n\nThe key words here are \"$20\\%$ *less than.*\" This means we need to apply a percentage decrease, decreasing the original number by $20\\%.$\n\nTo solve this problem, we follow three steps.\n\n- **Step 1.** Subtract the decrease from $100\\%.$\n\nTo find a number $20\\%$ *less than* $250,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% - 20\\% = 80\\%.\n$$\n\nThis means that our new number is $80\\%$ of $250.$\n\n- **Step 2.** Convert the result to a decimal multiplier.\n\nNow, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n80\\% = \\dfrac{80}{100} = 0.8\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the product of the multiplier and the original number.\n\nSo, to find a number that is $20\\%$ less than $250,$ we multiply $250$ by $0.8{:}$\n\n$$\n0.8 \\cdot 250 = 200\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $20\\%$ less than $250$ is $200.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186230317 1075 #edb/ref 17592186230318 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186230317 1075 #edb/ref 17592186230319 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186230317 1075 #edb/ref 17592186230320 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186230317 1075 #edb/ref 17592186230321 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186230317 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508401 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186230317 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508565 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186230317 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508588 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186230317 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508656 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186230317 1078 #edb/ref 17592186508588 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186231406 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508963 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186231573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186230307 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186231575 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186231575 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-3,3]$, where $f$ is $6$-periodic and even. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186231575 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n$y^{″} + 3y = f(x)$\non $[- 3, 3]$, where $f$ is $6$-periodic and even. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution $y_{p}$?\n$y_{p}(x) =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186231575 1074 "Because $f$ is $6$-periodic on $[-3,3]$, we have $L=3$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186283994 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283243 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186283994 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508670 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186284487 1074 "The full expression describing all points on our hyperbolic cylinder is\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{8}=1,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{8}=1\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola centered at $(3,-6).$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186284487 1074 "The full expression describing all points on our hyperbolic cylinder is\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{8}=1,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{8}=1}.\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola centered at $(3,-6).$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186284488 1074 "The full expression describing all points on our parabolic cylinder is\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}=3y,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:(x-1)^{2}=3y\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola with a vertex at $(1,0).$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186284488 1074 "The full expression describing all points on our parabolic cylinder is\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}=3y,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:(x-1)^{2}=3y}.\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola with a vertex at $(1,0).$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186357256 1078 #edb/ref 17592186355077 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186357256 1078 #edb/ref 17592186355461 13194139535463 17592186045417 false] [17592186372854 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-4,4]$, where $f$ is $8$-periodic and odd. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186372854 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-4,4]$, where $f$ is $8$-periodic and odd. Which Fourier-series form is ap