3 17592186045417 true] [17592186508909 1078 #edb/ref 17592186508579 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508909 1079 #uuid "fbadbf3f-b18a-5a99-9826-e9ff4c997442" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508910 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508496 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508910 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508649 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508910 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508688 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508910 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508747 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508910 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508793 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508910 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508910 1077 "selection" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508910 1078 #edb/ref 17592186508688 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508910 1079 #uuid "fc90adbc-d567-5552-83aa-263b9e6bc19f" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508911 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508865 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508911 1069 "q-118712" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508911 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508911 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x+2}{x+1})}^{2x+2}.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508911 1072 #uuid "00868e65-0caa-53d3-bac1-8c3f5e5b6143" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508911 1074 "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x+2}{x+1})}^{2x+2}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x+2}{x+1})}^{2x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{(x+1)+1}{x+1})}^{2x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{x+1})}^{2(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $x+1=n.$ Therefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x+2}{x+1})}^{2x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{x+1})}^{2(x+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508912 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508878 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508912 1069 "q-344853" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508912 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508912 1071 "![](images/9e/9ea7b23d8dd14a0ed092152592800022400ee73e7b371146f5e972900f2c2f29.png)\n\nA community organizer wants to install a border around a walkway made up of a rectangle and two semicircles, as shown in the diagram. She has only $120\\,m$ of border material. The organizer wishes to maximize the area of the **rectangle**. Find $x+2r$ where $x$ and $2r$ are the dimensions of the rectangle that has the greatest area. Do not round the answer.\n\n$x+2r=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508912 1072 #uuid "06dbc133-9492-57ef-8ac9-643d61a66e1b" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508912 1074 "First, let $r$ be the radius of the semicircles (the same for both semicircles) and let $x$ be the length of the other side of the rectangle. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2rx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $120\\text{ m},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n120=\\pi r+\\pi r+2x=2\\pi r+2x.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $x$ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120 & =2\\pi r+2x \\\\ 2x & =120-2\\pi r \\\\ x & =60-\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2r(60-\\pi r) \\\\  & =120r-2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A,$ so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =120-4\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ 120-4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =120 \\\\ r & =\\frac{30}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=\\frac{30}{\\pi }$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-4\\pi \\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{30}{\\pi })=-4\\pi <0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{30}{\\pi })$ is negative, we conclude that $r=\\frac{30}{\\pi }$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nFinally, when $r=\\frac{30}{\\pi }$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =60-\\pi (\\frac{30}{\\pi }) \\\\  & =60-30 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the largest area are $x=30$ and $2r=\\frac{60}{\\pi },$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nx+2r=30+\\frac{60}{\\pi }=\\frac{30\\pi +60}{\\pi }.\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508913 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508831 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508913 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508855 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508913 1069 "q-238044" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508913 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508913 1071 "Consider the following system of equations. \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x+y=8 \\\\ 8x-7y=25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $y$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $y,$ multiply the \n{{field-1}} \nequation by \n{{field-2}}, \nand add the equations." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508913 1072 #uuid "09338f11-6e50-5f7e-baa9-764c66288045" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508913 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $1,$ while in the second equation it is $-7.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $7,$ then the coefficient of $y$ will turn into $7,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $-7$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,-5x+y=8\\,)\\,\\times (7) \\\\ 8x-7y=25\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-35x+7y=56 \\\\ 8x-7y=25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-35x+\\cancel{7y} & =56 \\\\ 8x-\\cancel{7y} & =25 \\\\ (-35x+8x) & =(56+25) \\\\ -27x & =81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $y,$ multiply the $\\text{first}$ equation by $7,$ and add the equations*." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508914 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508839 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508914 1069 "q-112148" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508914 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508914 1071 "The number of subscribers to a video channel is given by $S(t)=5\\cdot (2.4)^{t},$ where $t$ is the time in days since the channel was opened. How many subscribers will the channel have after a week? Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508914 1072 #uuid "1571510a-8d3e-5007-9b28-e17c3cb5bde3" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508914 1074 "To get the number of subscribers to the video channel after a week ($7$ days), we evaluate the function at $t=7.$ Substituting $t=7$ into the function $S(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(7) & =5\\cdot (2.4)^{7} \\\\  & ≈2\\,293.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, after a week, the video channel will have $2\\,293$ subscribers." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508915 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508871 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508915 1069 "q-347235" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508915 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508915 1071 "![](images/ab/ab0416aeff61e4916acc1765a7931da0a41a64ba9d0ac4c05a322de6a1ed431b.png)\n\nA box whose depth $z$ is equal to half of its height $y$ has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the top and bottom costs $$4$ per square meter, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$1$ per square meter. The box must have a volume of $36\\,{m}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $z$ for the box that has the smallest production cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}} units" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508915 1072 #uuid "180b48d4-1e2c-527c-b442-f02b5ba67824" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508915 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $z$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =4\\cdot 2xz+1\\cdot 2xy+1\\cdot 2yz \\\\  & =8xz+2xy+2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $36\\,{m}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot z\\cdot y \\\\ 36 & =xz(2z) \\\\ 36 & =2x{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $x$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x{z}^{2} & =36 \\\\ x & =\\frac{18}{{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =8xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =8\\,(\\frac{18}{{z}^{2}})\\,z+2\\,(\\frac{18}{{z}^{2}})\\,(2z)+2(2z)z \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{z}+\\frac{72}{z}+4{z}^{2} \\\\  & =4{z}^{2}+\\frac{216}{z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(z)=8z-\\frac{216}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ 8z-\\frac{216}{{z}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 8{z}^{3} & =216 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =27 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=3$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(z)=8+\\frac{432}{{z}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(3)=8+16=24>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(3)$ is positive, we conclude that $z=3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(z).$\n\nTherefore, the production cost is the smallest when $z=3$ units." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508916 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508888 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508916 1069 "q-345710" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508916 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508916 1071 "![](images/9d/9d3a3f70571b0bfca1d4c5251113b915a8061955cb6f48c310d1939571c14e47.png)\n\nConsider a box with a surface area of $432$ square units whose depth $z$ is twice its height $y.$ Find the value of $y$ that maximizes the volume of the box. Do not round the answer.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}} units" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508916 1072 #uuid "1a6b4914-aa75-5cc3-80a2-4dde99e3c7aa" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508916 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $y$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=x\\cdot y\\cdot z=x\\cdot y\\cdot (2y)=2x{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The surface area is $432$ square units. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xy+2xz+2yz \\\\ 432 & =2xy+2xz+2yz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $z=2y,$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}432 & =2xy+2x(2y)+2y(2y) \\\\ 432 & =2xy+4xy+4{y}^{2} \\\\ 432 & =6xy+4{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{72}{y}-\\frac{2}{3}y\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into the expression $V=2x{y}^{2}$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(y) & =2{y}^{2}(\\frac{72}{y}-\\frac{2}{3}y) \\\\  & =144y-\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=144-4{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 144-4{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =36 \\\\ y & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the side length $y$ must be positive.\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $y=6$ gives a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=-8y\\,⟹\\,{V}^{″}(6)=-48<0,\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(6)$ is negative, we conclude that this is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the box with the largest possible volume has height $y=6$ units." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508917 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508817 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508917 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508834 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508917 1069 "q-238035" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508917 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508917 1071 "Consider the following system of equations. \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}10x-3y=36 \\\\ 2x+5y=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $x$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $x,$ multiply the \n{{field-1}} \nequation by \n{{field-2}}, \nand add the equations." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508917 1072 #uuid "2a68ca7c-dc16-5d70-b5c4-e7c98a96c8e2" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508917 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $10,$ while in the second equation it is $2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-5,$ then the coefficient of $x$ will turn into $-10,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $10$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}10x-3y=36 \\\\ (\\,2x+5y=-4\\,)\\,\\times (-5)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}10x-3y=36 \\\\ -10x-25y=20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{10x}-3y & =36 \\\\ -\\cancel{10x}-25y & =20 \\\\ (-3y-25y) & =(36+20) \\\\ -28y & =56 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*Multiply the $\\text{second}$ equation by $-5,$ and add the equations*." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508918 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508842 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508918 1069 "q-164423" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508918 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508918 1071 "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the elliptic cylinder $(x+3)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1?$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508918 1072 #uuid "2ff3c43f-106f-5820-9db0-287d2d56d9d9" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508918 1074 "The full expression describing all points on our elliptic cylinder is\n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:(x+3)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1\\}\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=1\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse centered at $(-3,0)$ with semi-axes of lengths $1$ and $5.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508919 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508815 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508919 1069 "q-18398" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508919 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508919 1071 "![](images/b0/b024db5e134e25212b44cef850118a6823148b01f782c67dccc42f12b82e3854.png)\n\nA piece of cardboard measures $8\\,cm\\times 6\\,cm.$ A tray of height $y\\,\\text{cm}$ is formed by cutting a $y\\times y\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}$ square from each of the corners and folding up the sides, as shown in the diagram. Determine the height of the box that gives the maximum possible volume. Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508919 1072 #uuid "35d71232-f38b-500b-9a6f-4af0bdca3a38" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508919 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x,y,$ and $z$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the width $x$ and the depth $z$ of the tray are given by\n\n$$\nx=8-2y,\\,z=6-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0$ and $z>0,$ we must have that $8-2y>0$ and $6-2y>0$, hence $y<3.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<3.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =xyz \\\\  & =(8-2y)(y)(6-2y) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-28{y}^{2}+48y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-56y+48,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-56y+48 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-14y+12 & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{7\\pm \\sqrt{13}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $\\frac{7+\\sqrt{13}}{3}$ since we already know that $0<y<3.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $\\frac{7-\\sqrt{13}}{3}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-56.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(\\frac{7-\\sqrt{13}}{3})$ is negative, we conclude that $\\frac{7-\\sqrt{13}}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the stationary point $y=\\frac{7-\\sqrt{13}}{3}≈1.13$ gives the maximum possible volume." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508920 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508864 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508920 1069 "q-63874" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508920 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508920 1071 "Olivia bought a home for $$150\\,000.$ The value of the home declined by $16%$ annually. To the nearest dollar, what was the value of the home after $4$ years?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508920 1072 #uuid "38f4d55c-c716-54e2-ab1e-afc12e3f81f0" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508920 1074 "The number $16%$ (expressed as a decimal) is equal to $0.16.$ Therefore, a decrease of $16%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of:\n\n$$\n1-0.16=0.84\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ year, value of the home was $150\\,000\\cdot (0.84)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, value of the home was $150\\,000\\cdot (0.84)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, value of the home was  $150\\,000\\cdot (0.84)^{3}$ dollars.\n\n- After $4$ years, value of the home was $150\\,000\\cdot (0.84)^{4}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the last expression gives:\n\n$$\n150\\,000\\cdot (0.84)^{4}≈74\\,681\n$$\n\nSo, the home was worth $$74\\,681$ (to the nearest dollar) after $4$ years." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508847 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508921 1069 "q-345016" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508921 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508921 1071 "![](images/0e/0ef7a2645946099b10772fdc3fee46c582ad43debb0cb53041c46aa99c203ab1.png)\n\nSofia is designing a park that is made up of a rectangle and two semicircles, as shown in the diagram. The area of the park must be $16\\,{km}^{2}.$ What should be the radius $r$ if Sofia wants a park with the smallest possible perimeter? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{km}$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508921 1072 #uuid "3990dbc6-49ea-5002-bf6b-028dcf68d1bf" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508921 1074 "We let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector (the same for both semicircles) and let $x$ be the other side of the rectangle. Then, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2x+\\pi r+\\pi r \\\\  & =2x+2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $16\\text{ km},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n16=2rx+\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $2x$ the subject of the constraint equation and taking into account that $r>0,$ we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16 & =2rx+\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 2rx & =16-\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{16}{r}-\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{16}{r}-\\pi r+2\\pi r \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{r}+\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. Computing the derivative gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(r) & =-\\frac{16}{{r}^{2}}+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{16}{{r}^{2}}+\\pi  & =0 \\\\ \\pi  & =\\frac{16}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{16}{\\pi } \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{\\pi }} \\\\ r & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative ${P}^{″}(r)=\\frac{32}{{r}^{3}}>0,$ the stationary point $r=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,\\text{km}$ is a minimum." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508922 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508898 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508922 1069 "q-347239" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508922 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508922 1071 "![](images/61/61e7943162b7df06af1d6ba805630268e872031e1cb27b566810ee7fc22e6bbc.png)\n\nA piece of cardboard measures $18\\,ft\\times 18\\,ft.$ A tray of height $y\\,\\text{ft}$ is formed by cutting a $y\\times y\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}$ square from each of the corners and folding up the sides, as shown in the diagram. Determine the height $y$ of the box that gives the maximum possible volume.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft}$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508922 1072 #uuid "3a8692c9-b01b-529e-b66c-5ef584b340c0" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508922 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x,y,$ and $z$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the width $x$ and the depth $z$ of the tray are given by\n\n$$\nx=18-2y,\\,z=18-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0$ and $z>0,$ we must have that $18-2y>0$ and $18-2y>0$, hence $y<9.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<9.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =xyz \\\\  & =(18-2y)(y)(18-2y) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-72{y}^{2}+324y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-144y+324,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-144y+324 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-12y+27 & =0 \\\\ y & =3,\\,9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $9$ since we already know that $0<y<9.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $3$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-144.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(3)$ is negative, we conclude that $3$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the height of the box with the maximum volume is $y=3\\text{ ft}.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508923 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508850 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508923 1069 "q-171945" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508923 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508923 1071 "The perimeter of the rectangle $ABCD$ is $32.$ If $AB=3BC,$ then its area is $A=${{field-1}}." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508923 1072 #uuid "404f0ec1-ce52-56bd-b28f-4f5a47723b39" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508923 1074 "Let $BC=x.$\n\nSince the perimeter of the rectangle is $p=32$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AB+BC+CD+DA \\\\ p & =2(AB)+2(BC) \\\\ 32 & =2(3x)+2(x) \\\\ 32 & =6x+2x \\\\ 8x & =32 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain $BC=4$ and $AB=3\\cdot 4=12.$\n\nTherefore, the area of a rectangle $ABCD$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =AB\\cdot BC \\\\  & =12\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508924 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508838 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508924 1069 "q-78293" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508924 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508924 1071 "![](images/96/96b7c8e8f6d1203327271f4960bde8804315f0f4ac6846beeb4a248af8b07b1a.png)\n\nUsing the graphical method, find the solutions $(x,y)$ of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=x-1 \\\\ y={x}^{2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508924 1072 #uuid "43f4b257-b0e8-5f30-8dc0-cac9bfb6fbb7" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508924 1074 "The solutions $(x,y)$ of the system correspond to the intersection points of the two curves.\n\n![](images/82/82ec1e7cdbe6275bc4fac3cdf32e07eb989570d82b66bb5a980f25355293e3f9.png)\n\nFrom the graph, the curves intersect at \n\n$\\,$ $(-1,-2)\\,$ and $\\,(2,1).$ \n\nTherefore, these are the solutions of the corresponding system." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508925 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508830 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508925 1069 "q-117067" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508925 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508925 1071 "![](images/bf/bf3beb1908abd2d626d4b49efb010f3ac5d7cb82fec7e22abd13fbc457c7202d.png)\n\nConsider a box with a square base with side lengths $x$ and height $y,$ as shown above. The volume of the box is $729$ cubic units. Given that the surface area of the box has been minimized, find the value of $x.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508925 1072 #uuid "445bb805-c2f6-5166-9dd9-5663cfa2ac32" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508925 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2{x}^{2}+4xy\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is exactly $729$ cubic units. So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot x\\cdot y \\\\ 729 & ={x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =729 \\\\ y & =\\frac{729}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2{x}^{2}+4xy \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+4x\\cdot \\frac{729}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+\\frac{2\\,916}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=4x-\\frac{2\\,916}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 4x-\\frac{2\\,916}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{2\\,916}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 4{x}^{3} & =2\\,916 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =729 \\\\ x & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=9$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(x)=4+\\frac{5\\,832}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(9)=4+8=12>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(9)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=9$ is indeed a minimum of $S(x).$\n\nTherefore, the box with the smallest possible surface area has side length $x=9$ units." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508926 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508826 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508926 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508827 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508926 1069 "q-238082" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508926 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508926 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+13y=21 \\\\ 2x+6y=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $(x,y)$ to this system is $($\n{{field-1}}$\\,,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508926 1072 #uuid "4775db8b-54b5-507a-83b4-6fee15253323" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508926 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the  first  equation is $6,$ while in the second equation it is $2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-3,$ then the $x$-coefficient will become $-6,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $6$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+13y=21 \\\\ (\\,2x+6y=12\\,)\\,\\times (-3)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+13y=21 \\\\ -6x-18y=-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{6x}+13y & =21 \\\\ -\\cancel{6x}-18y & =-36 \\\\ (13y-18y) & =(21-36) \\\\ -5y & =-15 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=3.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+13y & =21 \\\\ 6x+13(3) & =21 \\\\ 6x+39 & =21 \\\\ 6x & =-18 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-3,\\,y=3,$ which can be expressed as $(-3,3).$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508927 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508886 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508927 1069 "q-170159" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508927 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508927 1071 "Find the solution $(p,q)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3p+2q=11 \\\\ 5p+4q=19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $q.$*" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508927 1072 #uuid "4c472f9d-cfda-5414-a840-ee6d18544bcc" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508927 1074 "Notice that the $q$-coefficient in the first equation is $2,$ while in the second equation it is $4.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $-2,$ then the $q$-coefficient will become $-4,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,3p+2q=11\\,)\\,\\times (-2) \\\\ 5p+4q=19\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-6p-4q=-22 \\\\ 5p+4q=19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $q$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}-6p-\\cancel{4q} & =-22 \\\\ 5p+\\cancel{4q} & =19 \\\\ (-6p+5p) & =(-22+19) \\\\ -p & =-3 \\\\ p & =3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $p=3.$ However, we still need to find $q.$ To do this, we substitute $p=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p+2q & =11 \\\\ 3(3)+2q & =11 \\\\ 9+2q & =11 \\\\ 2q & =2 \\\\ q & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=3,\\,q=1,$ which can be expressed as $(3,1).$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508928 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508856 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508928 1069 "q-344938" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508928 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508928 1071 "![](images/60/60e04faa7448d351706ee1af70a8620590ca16e0debce7dd2e58631462594132.png)\n\nConsider all circular sectors $DEF$ with area $A=144$ square units. Determine the smallest possible perimeter $P$ of such a circular sector. Do not round the answer.\n\n$P=$\n{{field-1}}\nunits" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508928 1072 #uuid "4d662c12-9d76-5b11-86a1-8fd56f6d8729" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508928 1074 "We let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. Then, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $144$ square units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n144={r}^{2}\\frac{\\theta }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation and taking into account that $r>0,$ we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}144 & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{288}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2r+r\\theta  \\\\  & =2r+r\\cdot \\frac{288}{{r}^{2}} \\\\  & =2r+\\frac{288}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. Computing the derivative gives \n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(r)=2-\\frac{288}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{288}{{r}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{r}^{2} & =288 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =144 \\\\ r & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=12$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative ${P}^{″}(r)=\\frac{576}{{r}^{3}}>0$, the stationary point $r=12$ is a minimum.\n\nFinally, when $r=12$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\frac{288}{{r}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{288}{144} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe dimensions of the circular sector with the smallest perimeter are $r=12$ and $\\theta =2.$ Therefore, the smallest perimeter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2r+r\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot 12+12\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =24+24 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508929 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508906 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508929 1069 "q-218594" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508929 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508929 1071 "If $f(x)=A\\cdot {2}^{x}$ and $f(6)=192,$ then $A=${{field-1}}." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508929 1072 #uuid "4fda74bd-4783-5b76-9154-8ddd3936c772" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508929 1074 "We substitute $x=6$ and $f(x)=192$ into the expression for $f(x)$ and then solve for $A,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}192 & =A\\cdot {2}^{6} \\\\ \\frac{192}{{2}^{6}} & =A \\\\ \\frac{192}{64} & =A \\\\ 3 & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=3.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508821 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508930 1069 "q-51205" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508930 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508930 1071 "![](images/59/59949e4ba7818ea08d4613fee703a2c973ffa4953c820f04c1ae9279147dad56.png)\n\nConsider all possible circular sectors $MON$ with perimeter $P=180$ units. What is the radius $r$ of the circular sector with the largest possible area?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508930 1072 #uuid "500619bc-56ac-575b-907b-2407a9ecc2e7" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508930 1074 "First,  let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $180$ units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n180=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{180-2r}{r}=\\frac{180}{r}-2.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(r) & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}(\\frac{180}{r}-2) \\\\  & =90r-{r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A,$ so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(r)=90-2r.\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ 90-2r & =0 \\\\ r & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=45$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(45)=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(45)$ is negative, we conclude that $r=45$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508825 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508873 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508909 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508931 1069 "q-352894" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508931 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508931 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508931 1072 #uuid "5753a7d8-bd1a-52e7-a471-c1acec6a4f75" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508931 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{2x})}^{′}-2(u{e}^{2x}) & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x}-2u{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-2x}({x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-2x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-2x}({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{-2x}({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-2x}({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\int {e}^{-2x}({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508932 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508823 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508932 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508843 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508932 1069 "q-238051" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508932 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508932 1071 "Consider the following system of equations. \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3p+2q=11 \\\\ p+q=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $q$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $q,$ multiply the \n{{field-1}} \nequation by \n{{field-2}}, \nand add the equations." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508932 1072 #uuid "5ba7c176-1d51-52ad-ad1b-a588df23024a" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508932 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $q$ in the first equation is $2,$ while in the second equation it is $1.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-2,$ then the coefficient of $q$ will turn into $-2,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $2$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3p+2q=11 \\\\ (\\,p+q=4\\,)\\,\\times (-2)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3p+2q=11 \\\\ -2p-2q=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $q$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}3p+\\cancel{2q} & =11 \\\\ -2p-\\cancel{2q} & =-8 \\\\ (3p-2p) & =(11-8) \\\\ p & =3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $q,$ multiply the $\\text{second}$ equation by $-2,$ and add the equations*." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508933 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508880 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508933 1069 "q-62054" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508933 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508933 1071 "The daily milk production on Debbie's farm has decreased at a rate of $12%$ every month. If the daily milk production was $1\\,130$ liters initially, then which function correctly represents $p(t),$ the daily milk production after $t$ months?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508933 1072 #uuid "5be49d8c-c7c0-5d12-b018-ff8157121a39" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508933 1074 "The formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\np(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=1\\,130$ is the initial daily milk production.\n\n- $r=0.12$ is the decay rate ($12%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in months.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $p$ is\n\n$$\np(t)=1\\,130\\cdot (1-0.12)^{t}=1\\,130\\cdot (0.88)^{t}.\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508934 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508874 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508934 1069 "q-81624" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508934 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508934 1071 "![](images/5c/5cd38fb898d5cc3fcef43ae6397b02f22fbd06cbe598263fe77ce6b936c16bae.png)\n\nA box has a square base with side lengths $x$ and height $y,$ as shown above. The surface area of the box is $216$ square units. Given that the volume of the box has been maximized, find the value of $x.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508934 1072 #uuid "5c2829d5-9e3f-5d96-8caf-fe7beef5d605" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508934 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the volume\n\n$$\nV=x\\cdot x\\cdot y={x}^{2}y\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The surface area is $216$ square units. So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\,\\text{(area of the base) }+4\\,\\text{(area of  one side) } \\\\ 216 & =2{x}^{2}+4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation: \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{54}{x}-\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=\\frac{54}{x}-\\frac{x}{2}$ in the expression $V={x}^{2}y,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(x) & ={x}^{2}(\\frac{54}{x}-\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\  & =54x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(x)=54-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 54-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =36 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step we only considered the positive root because the side length $x$ must be positive. \n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=6$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(x)=-3x\\,⟹\\,{V}^{″}(6)=-18<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(6)$ is negative, we conclude that $x=6$ is indeed a maximum of $V(x).$\n\nTherefore, the box with the largest possible volume has side length $x=6$ units." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508935 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508837 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508935 1069 "q-315628" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508935 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508935 1071 "![](images/85/8548c87f15edc7e7c903c753767f12bd6f4c6f0294ff07734659583907f2ea4a.png)\n\nA box whose width $x$ is $2$ times its depth $z$ has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the top and bottom costs $$5$ per square foot, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$2$ per square foot. The box must have a volume of $180\\,{ft}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $z$ for the box that has the smallest production cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}} units" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508935 1072 #uuid "5f708375-cdc1-53f8-8bb4-b17c1643502c" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508935 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $z$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =5\\cdot 2xz+2\\cdot 2xy+2\\cdot 2yz \\\\  & =10xz+4xy+4yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $180\\,{ft}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot z\\cdot y \\\\ 180 & =2{z}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{z}^{2}y & =180 \\\\ y & =\\frac{90}{{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =10xz+4xy+4yz \\\\  & =10(2z)z+4(2z)(\\frac{90}{{z}^{2}})+4\\,(\\frac{90}{{z}^{2}})\\,z \\\\  & =20{z}^{2}+\\frac{720}{z}+\\frac{360}{z} \\\\  & =20{z}^{2}+\\frac{1080}{z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(z)=40z-\\frac{1080}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ 40z-\\frac{1080}{{z}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 40{z}^{3} & =1080 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =27 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=3$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(z)=40+\\frac{2160}{{z}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(3)=40+80=120>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(3)$ is positive, we conclude that $z=3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(z).$\n\nTherefore, the production cost is the smallest when $z=3$ units." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508936 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508820 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508936 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508857 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508936 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508866 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508936 1069 "q-337475" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508936 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508936 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508936 1072 #uuid "5fdf7d2e-21a5-5a00-afb9-716691a79836" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508936 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =6{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x=\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =6{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\  & =6{e}^{-3x}(\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-3x}({e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =3-3{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =3-3{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3-3{e}^{-6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =3x+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =3x{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508885 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508937 1069 "q-346825" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508937 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508937 1071 "![](images/c6/c652b4ce5aa631211824e1f05160bf870c29d3c4d54ea54c29c627aaf7754eea.png)\n\nConsider a box with width $x,$ height $y,$ and depth $z,$ as shown above. The depth of the box is four times its height. Given that the box's volume is $800$ cubic units, what is the smallest possible surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ of the box? Do not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} square units" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508937 1072 #uuid "64a50494-969c-52da-a2bf-9af25982f51d" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508937 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $y$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is $800$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 800 & =xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $z=4y,$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n800=4x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $x$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x{y}^{2} & =800 \\\\ x & =\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2x(4y)+2xy+2y(4y) \\\\  & =8xy+2xy+8{y}^{2} \\\\  & =10xy+8{y}^{2} \\\\  & =10(\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}})y+8{y}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2000}{y}+8{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(y)=16y-\\frac{2000}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 16y-\\frac{2000}{{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 16{y}^{3} & =2000 \\\\ {y}^{3} & =125 \\\\ y & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $y=5$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(y)=16+\\frac{4000}{{y}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(5)=16+32=48>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(5)$ is positive, we conclude that $y=5$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(5) & =8(5)^{2}+\\frac{2000}{5} \\\\  & =600\\,\\text{square units}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508938 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508812 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508938 1069 "q-78288" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508938 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508938 1071 "![](images/9f/9fbc46d5a81532e274d9f59832db14730169905d383dccb61d0039b2bc89ab31.png)\n\nUsing the graphical method, find the solutions $(x,y)$ of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508938 1072 #uuid "688b04b8-3e75-5482-8d9f-399abc4be90f" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508938 1074 "The solutions $(x,y)$ of the system correspond to the intersection points of the two curves.\n\n![](images/83/83a2271a5f0620bcb97698d8b04367e6935f1e8cd6c25f6d2538aeb411c75a9f.png)\n\nWe see from the graph that the curves intersect at \n\n$\\,$ $(-3,2)\\,$ and $\\,(3,2).$ \n\nTherefore, these are the solutions of the corresponding system." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508939 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508868 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508939 1069 "q-53279" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508939 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508939 1071 "![](images/54/547debca3e0215ebad8db2c525f9d1b0d435379c081073013cdbfd52b1b6c0f9.png)\n\nThe area of the rectangular court shown above is $56\\,{\\text{yd}}^{2}.$ What is the value of $x?$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508939 1072 #uuid "6c1d9607-760c-5f72-b720-f7fb112fffe1" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508939 1074 "The area of a rectangle is given by \n\n$$\nA=a\\cdot b,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are the lengths of the sides.\n\nFrom the figure, we obtain that \n\n$$\na=2x-6\\,yd,\\,A=56\\,{yd}^{2},\\,b=7\\,\\text{yd}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =a\\cdot b \\\\ 56 & =(2x-6)\\cdot 7 \\\\ 56 & =14x-42 \\\\ 98 & =14x \\\\ x & =7\\,yd.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508940 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508862 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508940 1069 "q-344791" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508940 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508940 1071 "![](images/5c/5cd71fa90a1213292961960facf049a1333beb63abeb53f08ba18173281fb330.png)\n\nConsider all possible circular sectors $ABC$ with perimeter $P=80$ units. Determine the largest possible area of such a circular sector. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe maximum area is\n{{field-1}}\nsquare units." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508940 1072 #uuid "6e590d69-9e57-5a7b-a44f-da12f0280772" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508940 1074 "First, let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $80$ units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n80=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{80-2r}{r}=\\frac{80}{r}-2.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(r) & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}(\\frac{80}{r}-2) \\\\  & =40r-{r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A$, so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(r)=40-2r.\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ 40-2r & =0 \\\\ r & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we confirm that $r=20$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(20)=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(20)$ is negative, we conclude that $r=20$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nTherefore, the circular sector of the largest area will have a radius of $20,$ and the area is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =40r-{r}^{2} \\\\  & =40(20)-(20)^{2} \\\\  & =400.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508941 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508887 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508941 1069 "q-123878" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508941 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508941 1071 "![](images/6d/6d5410407be54a897353c75cd9d780fcd7c1373fd2371bc4e0ca0f737ba58308.png)\n\nUsing the graphical method, find the solution $(x,y)$ of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=f(x) \\\\ y=g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508941 1072 #uuid "6f0c806d-87b9-56dc-bfb8-0487c34983b3" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508941 1074 "The solutions $(x,y)$ of the system correspond to the intersection point of the two curves.\n\n![](images/3b/3bb3ee0e430c4ad86f7378d21285ade9c911a2d3bb91c7d017c65e49ee630097.png)\n\nFrom the graph, the curves intersect only at $(1,3).$ Therefore, this is the solution of the corresponding system." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508942 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508904 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508942 1069 "q-78306" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508942 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508942 1071 "![](images/b7/b7ba17234415854d4d07721715ad411db6fb6c87e840193861a9b35244057416.png)\n\nUsing the graphical method, find the solutions $(x,y)$ of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=f(x) \\\\ y=g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508942 1072 #uuid "74d5d63f-ce92-5d6a-9703-105dc97e875a" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508942 1074 "The solutions $(x,y)$ of the system correspond to the intersection points of the two curves.\n\n![](images/03/0361be3b806f4d7fdff73d913e7926ec52658652eaf88eed25bec41d5a340c06.png)\n\nFrom the graph, the curves intersect only at $(2,5).$ Therefore, this is the solution of the corresponding system." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508943 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508832 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508943 1069 "q-86444" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508943 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508943 1071 "![](images/c4/c4bbdc87980efd87c64fdb8fa693078817e16bbff136f94a2ac4dfc2c651b17d.png)\n\nConsider a box with a surface area of $48$ square units whose width $x$ is twice its depth $z.$ Given that the volume of the box has been maximized, find the value of $z.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508943 1072 #uuid "761b830c-0a32-54fa-8bad-182a530f7511" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508943 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x,y,$ and $z$ such as the volume\n\n$$\nV=x\\cdot y\\cdot z=2z\\cdot y\\cdot z=2{z}^{2}y\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The surface area is $48$ square units. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xy+2xz+2yz \\\\ 48 & =2xy+2xz+2yz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $x=2z,$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}48 & =2(2z)y+2(2z)z+2yz \\\\ 48 & =4yz+4{z}^{2}+2yz \\\\ 48 & =6zy+4{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{8}{z}-\\frac{2z}{3}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into the expression $V=2{z}^{2}y,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(z) & =2{z}^{2}(\\frac{8}{z}-\\frac{2z}{3}) \\\\  & =16z-\\frac{4{z}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(z)=16-4{z}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ 16-4{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =4 \\\\ z & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step we only considered the positive root because the side length $z$ must be positive. \n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=2$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(z)=-8z\\,⟹\\,{V}^{″}(2)=-16<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(2)$ is negative, we conclude that $z=2$ is indeed a maximum of $V(z).$\n\nTherefore, the box with the largest possible volume has the depth $z=2$ units." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508944 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508853 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508944 1069 "q-61793" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508944 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508944 1071 "The population of a certain animal species is declining at a rate of $3%$ per year. If the species' initial population was $5\\,000$ when the observations started, then which function correctly represents $y(t),$ the population of the species after $t$ years?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508944 1072 #uuid "76eb003d-42ac-559c-8f1c-f5ce54d3972f" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508944 1074 "The formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=5\\,000$ is the initial population.\n\n- $r=0.03$ is the decay rate ($3%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=5\\,000\\cdot (1-0.03)^{t}=5\\,000\\cdot (0.97)^{t}.\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508945 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508902 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508945 1069 "q-164545" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508945 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508945 1071 "Identify the following surface:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=-z,\\,-\\infty <y<\\infty .\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508945 1072 #uuid "77084529-91b2-5a0f-94a4-b8df58237bc4" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508945 1074 "Recall that the equation of a parabolic cylinder whose axis is the $z$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py.\n$$\n\nThus, a parabolic cylinder whose axis is the $y$**-axis** is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4pz.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation is a parabolic cylinder whose axis is the $y$-axis and $p=-\\frac{1}{4}.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508946 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508892 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508946 1069 "q-344931" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508946 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508946 1071 "![](images/d2/d21626823e213a4c2d198f42e8e66d353364e722c1add41b35fcbc138ed8bff4.png)\n\nConsider all circular sectors $JKL$ with area $A=121$ square units. Determine the radius $r$ of the circular sector with the smallest possible perimeter. Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}\nunits" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508946 1072 #uuid "7c9b4d08-b984-5348-9fbd-0d702cd0c142" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508946 1074 "We let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. Then, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $121$ square units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n121=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation and taking into account that $r>0,$ we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}121 & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{242}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2r+r\\theta  \\\\  & =2r+r\\cdot \\frac{242}{{r}^{2}} \\\\  & =2r+\\frac{242}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(r)=2-\\frac{242}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{242}{{r}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{r}^{2} & =242 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =121 \\\\ r & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=11$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative ${P}^{″}(r)=\\frac{484}{{r}^{3}}>0,$ the stationary point $r=11$ is a minimum.\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circular sector with the smallest perimeter is $r=11$ units." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508947 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508849 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508947 1069 "q-85526" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508947 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508947 1071 "Due to acaricides, the population of ticks in a certain park decreases according to the expression $20\\,000\\cdot (0.7)^{t},$ where $t$ is the time, in weeks, since acaricides were first applied. What does the base of the exponential expression $(0.7)$ mean in this context?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508947 1072 #uuid "836a9482-113e-597c-80f8-a8ed0a6b01c8" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508947 1074 "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot (1-r)^{t},$ where:\n\n- $a=20\\,000$ represents the initial number of ticks.\n\n- $1-r=0.7,$ where $r$ is the decay rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in weeks, since the acaricides were first applied.\n\nSolving for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n1-r=0.7\\,⟹\\,r=0.3.\n$$\n\nNow, $0.3$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $30%.$ \n\nSo, the base of the exponential expression $0.7$ means that the population of ticks decreases by $30%$ every week." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508948 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508896 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508948 1069 "q-83831" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508948 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508948 1071 "For the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{4}-12{x}^{2}+x+1,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508948 1072 #uuid "87021664-1460-517e-b0da-ae45fc0bbb9e" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508948 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $1,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $4,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2$ and $\\pm 4.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. Listing those potential roots in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $6$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 3=6.$)" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508949 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508863 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508949 1069 "q-61704" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508949 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508949 1071 "Ralf is investigating the effect of a substance on some bacteria. In the beginning, there were $3\\,000$ bacteria. After introducing the substance, Ralf noticed that the number of bacteria reduces by $\\frac{1}{4}$ every minute.\n\nThe function $y(t)$ models the number of bacteria after $t$ minutes and is given by\n\n$$\ny(t)=3\\,000\\cdot {(\\text{___})}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWhat number is missing?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508949 1072 #uuid "87d542d6-aac4-513b-9fc9-107312f9fcdf" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508949 1074 "If the number of alive bacteria reduces by $\\frac{1}{4}$ every minute, then $\\frac{3}{4}$ of the bacteria remains every minute. So the decay factor is $\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=3\\,000$ is the initial number of bacteria.\n\n- $b=\\frac{3}{4}$ is the decay factor.\n\n- $t=$ the time, in minutes.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=3\\,000\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{t}.\n$$\n\nSo the missing number is $\\frac{3}{4}.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508950 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508833 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508950 1069 "q-64117" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508950 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508950 1071 "A scientist observes that the mass, in grams, of a particular substance decays exponentially according to the rule  $12\\cdot (0.5)^{t},$ where $t$ is the time, in hours, since the beginning of observations.  What does the factor $12$ mean in this context?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508950 1072 #uuid "8a686be2-6ff3-542b-aec0-e3ca1a18babf" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508950 1074 "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot {b}^{t},$ where we have the following:\n\n- $a=12$ represents the initial mass of the sample.\n\n- $b=0.5$ is the decay factor.\n\n- $t$ is the time since the beginning of the observations, in hours.\n\nSince $a=12,$ this indicates that the sample's mass at the beginning of the observations was  $12$ grams." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508951 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508845 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508951 1069 "q-85527" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508951 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508951 1071 "The daily number of customers to a particular cafe declines according to the expression $500\\cdot (0.83)^{t}$, where $t$ is the number of days since the local news reported that the cafe did not pass a health inspection. \nWhat does the base of the exponential expression $(0.83)$ mean in this context?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508951 1072 #uuid "8a688991-2eb3-5f8d-836d-4a680ceee3ff" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508951 1074 "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot (1-r)^{t},$ where:\n\n- $a=500$ represents the initial number of customers.\n\n- $1-r=0.83,$ where $r$ is the decay rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time in days since the local news reported that the cafe did not pass a health inspection.\n\nSolving for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n1-r=0.83\\,⟹\\,r=0.17.\n$$\n\nNow, $0.17$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $17%.$ \n\nSo, the base of the exponential expression $0.83$ means that the number of customers decreases by $17%$ every day." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508952 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508854 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508952 1069 "q-346764" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508952 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508952 1071 "![](images/1e/1e039fda5af235c4a9948853295846eaadb064fede888b84fdb5be552a35e3f1.png)\n\nConsider a box with width $x,$ height $y,$ and depth $z,$ as shown above. The depth of the box is one-third its width. Given that the box's volume is $288$ cubic units, what is the smallest possible surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ of the box? Do not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} square units" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508952 1072 #uuid "90ab0435-552f-51ca-b675-8d58a2c58837" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508952 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is $288$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 288 & =xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $z=\\frac{x}{3},$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n288=xy(\\frac{x}{3})=\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{3}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{3} & =288 \\\\ {x}^{2}y & =864 \\\\ y & =\\frac{864}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2x(\\frac{x}{3})+2x(\\frac{864}{{x}^{2}})+2(\\frac{864}{{x}^{2}})(\\frac{x}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1728}{x}+\\frac{576}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{2}+\\frac{2304}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=\\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{2304}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{2304}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}x & =\\frac{2304}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3} & =2304 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =1728 \\\\ x & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=12$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(x)=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{4608}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(12)=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}=4>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(12)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=12$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(12) & =\\frac{2}{3}(12)^{2}+\\frac{2304}{12} \\\\  & =96+192 \\\\  & =288\\,\\text{square units}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508953 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508875 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508953 1069 "q-127543" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508953 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508953 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3t-6})}^{t-2}.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508953 1072 #uuid "91b96bb8-d0ec-5384-8420-38fd513a4a78" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508953 1074 "Notice that as $t\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3t-6})}^{t-2}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nIn the given limit, we let $3t-6=n.$ Thus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3t-6 & =n \\\\ 3(t-2) & =n \\\\ t-2 & =\\frac{n}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3t-6})}^{t-2} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n/3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/3} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/3} \\\\  & ={e}^{1/3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508954 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508814 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508954 1069 "q-131070" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508954 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508954 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{{x}^{2}-1}, & x<1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+1}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[-5,1)$\n\nII. $[0,5]$\n\nIII. $[5,10]$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508954 1072 #uuid "91c6ce97-3ba3-5af9-8b62-e9a8d179a50b" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508954 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- The interval $[-5,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now, consider $[0,5].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{x(x-1)}{(x-1)(x+1)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x}{x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{1}{x+1})=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[0,5],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, consider $[5,10].$ In this interval $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[5,10].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508955 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508905 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508955 1069 "q-344681" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508955 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508955 1071 "![](images/f1/f17ce467f5bff34f0c04326d1a23ca8621f459571be8c98a745174a3d5587337.png)\n\nConsider all possible circular sectors $MON$ with perimeter $P=100$ units. Determine the angle $\\theta $ (in radians) of the circular sector with the largest possible area. Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}\nradians" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508955 1072 #uuid "9284b07a-9db4-5c4c-b135-110855ca0f14" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508955 1074 "First, let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $100$ units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n100=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{100-2r}{r}=\\frac{100}{r}-2.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(r) & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}(\\frac{100}{r}-2) \\\\  & =50r-{r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A$, so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(r)=50-2r.\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ 50-2r & =0 \\\\ r & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we confirm that $r=25$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(25)=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(25)$ is negative, we conclude that $r=25$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nFinally, when $r=25$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\frac{100}{r}-2 \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{25}-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508956 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508829 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508956 1069 "q-15089" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508956 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508956 1071 "![](images/c9/c9b79e710dda94e6c4d91a157317977f5b2388202bb63b08fcc325ea93f0453c.png)\n\nA gardener wants to create a flowerbed in the shape of a rectangle topped by a semicircle, as shown in the diagram. He needs the flowerbed to have an area of $\\frac{25}{9}\\,{cm}^{2}.$ What is the radius $r$ of the flowerbed with the smallest possible perimeter?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508956 1072 #uuid "92add898-4b49-5d01-bf37-847e609cbb26" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508956 1074 "We let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $x$ the side of the rectangle.\nThen, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=\\pi r+2x+2r.\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $\\frac{25}{9}$ square units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{9}=2rx+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $x$ the subject of the constraint equation and taking into account that $r>0,$ we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{25}{9} & =2rx+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2} \\\\ 2rx & =\\frac{25}{9}-\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{25}{18r}-\\frac{\\pi r}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\pi r+2x+2r \\\\  & =\\pi r+2(\\frac{25}{18r}-\\frac{\\pi r}{4})+2r \\\\  & =\\pi r+\\frac{25}{9r}-\\frac{\\pi r}{2}+2r \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2)r+\\frac{25}{9r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. Computing the derivative gives \n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(r)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2-\\frac{25}{9{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2-\\frac{25}{9{r}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 9{r}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{50}{9(\\pi +4)} \\\\ r & =\\frac{5}{3}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi +4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=\\frac{5}{3}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi +4}}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative ${P}^{″}(r)=\\frac{50}{9{r}^{3}}>0$, the stationary point $r=\\frac{5}{3}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi +4}}$ is a minimum." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508957 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508816 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508957 1069 "q-71584" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508957 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508957 1071 "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}?$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508957 1072 #uuid "95921cae-0a0b-5427-8528-229b03960d35" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508957 1074 "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{1}{3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ c(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =1 \\\\ 3c{x}^{2}+c{y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)$ can't be zero or negative. Therefore, we get a family of surfaces given by\n\n$$\n3c{x}^{2}+c{y}^{2}=1,\\,c\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508879 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508958 1069 "q-117209" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508958 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508958 1071 "![](images/d7/d77c003f88d428578c7f249feb4d0d086c8151c1ef21348d806a0baba751000d.png)\n\nConsider a box with width $x,$ height $y,$ and depth $z,$ as shown above. The width of the box is four times its height. Given that the box's volume is $800$ cubic units, what is the smallest possible surface area of the box?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508958 1072 #uuid "97252151-ba80-5f2a-b51f-786d26797ca5" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508958 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $y$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is exactly $800$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 800 & =xyz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $x=4y,$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n800=4z{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $z$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4z{y}^{2} & =800 \\\\ z & =\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2(4y)(\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}})+2(4y)y+2y(\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,600}{y}+8{y}^{2}+\\frac{400}{y} \\\\  & =8{y}^{2}+\\frac{2\\,000}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(y)=16y-\\frac{2\\,000}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 16y-\\frac{2\\,000}{{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 16{y}^{3}-2\\,000 & =0 \\\\ 16{y}^{3} & =2\\,000 \\\\ {y}^{3} & =125 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $y=5$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(y)=16+\\frac{4\\,000}{{y}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(5)=16+32=48>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(5)$ is positive, we conclude that $y=5$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(5) & =8(5)^{2}+\\frac{2\\,000}{5}=600.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508870 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508959 1069 "q-118714" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508959 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508959 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3x+1}{3x})}^{6x}.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508959 1072 #uuid "981ba9c5-75b9-5f37-b62a-2b2258f28bdb" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508959 1074 "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3x+1}{3x})}^{6x}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3x+1}{3x})}^{6x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3x})}^{2(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $3x=n.$ Therefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3x+1}{3x})}^{6x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3x})}^{2(3x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508828 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508960 1069 "q-117241" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508960 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508960 1071 "![](images/8c/8cb99cea56d8a57b607589305bfe225610c63504239296e0b6eb81ba220feec8.png)\n\nA piece of cardboard measures $6\\,in\\times 6\\,in.$ A tray of height $y\\,\\text{in}$ is formed by cutting a $y\\times y\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}$ square from each of the corners and folding up the sides, as shown in the diagram. Determine the height of the box that gives the maximum possible volume." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508960 1072 #uuid "9d40af61-e6e1-509f-9bd7-a155591f2abe" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508960 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV={x}^{2}y\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the side length $x$  of the tray is given by\n\n$$\nx=6-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0,$ we must have that $6-2y>0$ hence $y<3.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<3.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{2}y \\\\  & =(6-2y)^{2}y \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-24{y}^{2}+36y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-48y+36,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-48y+36 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y+3 & =0 \\\\ (y-3)(y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =3,\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $3$ since we already know that $0<y<3.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $1$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-48.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(1)=-24$ is negative, we conclude that $1$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the stationary point $y=1$ gives the maximum possible volume." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508961 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508907 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508961 1069 "q-117179" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508961 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508961 1071 "![](images/e0/e0a9d0aeba77f3ca2cd472b9c65df156394990a89a643e9a316ee662a52eb6b3.png)\n\nDavid wants to redesign his house, changing a small window for a big square window. The dimensions of the square window are shown in the figure above. What area covers the window?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508961 1072 #uuid "9d8646f3-f09f-5c98-8c8f-8b6c0f35dfe5" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508961 1074 "The area of a square with sides of length $a$ is given by \n\n$$\nA={a}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the figure, we obtain that $a=4\\,m.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={a}^{2}={4}^{2}=16\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508962 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508895 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508962 1069 "q-117071" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508962 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508962 1071 "Find the area of the rectangle $ABCD$ if its perimeter is $36$ and $BC=10.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508962 1072 #uuid "9fa4030e-f9e5-59f6-ba9d-65a980d49dfe" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508962 1074 "We can find the length of $AB$ by using the formula for the perimeter, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AB+BC+CD+DA \\\\ p & =2AB+2BC \\\\ 36 & =2AB+2(10) \\\\ 36 & =2AB+20 \\\\ 2AB & =16 \\\\ AB & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the rectangle $ABCD$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =AB\\cdot BC \\\\  & =10\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508963 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508858 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508963 1069 "q-344320" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508963 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508963 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)=6\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $4$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508963 1072 #uuid "a25da531-efbb-5c52-a54e-e70d588e0c03" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508963 1074 "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+5y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny(0)={y}_{c}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,y(1)={y}_{c}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+B(0)+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).$\n\n- Use $y(1)=6$ with $A=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ 6 & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+0 \\\\ B & =\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508867 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508964 1069 "q-45481" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508964 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508964 1071 "The price of a new bicycle is $$625.$ If the bicycle loses $\\frac{1}{5}$ of its value every year, what will be its value after $4$ years?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508964 1072 #uuid "a3a40784-83f5-5659-8e12-9e2f515c5795" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508964 1074 "Since the value of the bicycle reduces by $\\frac{1}{5}$ per year, $\\frac{4}{5}$ of its value remains every year. \n\nLet's calculate the value of the bicycle after every year:\n\n- After $1$ year, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot (\\frac{4}{5})$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{5})}^{3}$ dollars.\n\n- After $4$ years, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{5})}^{4}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the last expression, we get\n\n$$\n625\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{5})}^{4}=256.\n$$\n\nSo after $4$ years, the bicycle will be worth $$256.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508965 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508883 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508965 1069 "q-53149" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508965 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508965 1071 "![](images/f6/f6b58865cc7048560bc547ed03b95977d5f9ed013cb411991a82d7da140717f7.png)\n\nFind the area of the triangle $△ABC.$\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508965 1072 #uuid "a56eecfc-87ce-57b1-87a4-1fa356b28d07" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508965 1074 "The area of the triangle is defined as \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n where $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the base is \n\n$$\nb=AC=5\n$$\n\nand the corresponding height is\n\n$$\nh=BC=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 8}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{2} \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508966 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508841 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508966 1069 "q-117186" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508966 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508966 1071 "![](images/eb/ebb60aa69c324ef1bfc70bc325ff71eca70426225859350c4bbd8a58a82a2757.png)\n\nThe parking lot in a market has a rectangular form whose dimensions are shown in the figure above. What is the area of the parking lot?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508966 1072 #uuid "a60833b6-ee33-5277-9b1d-5d1fccd3856a" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508966 1074 "The area of a rectangle is given by \n\n$$\nA=a\\cdot b,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are the lengths of the sides.\n\nFrom the figure, we obtain that \n\n$$\na=200\\,m,\\,b=60\\,m.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,  the area of the field is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =a\\cdot b=200\\cdot 60=12\\,000\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508967 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508872 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508967 1069 "q-112449" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508967 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508967 1071 "Due to a quarantine initiated in March $2020$, the number of reservations per night in a hotel decreased by one-quarter each month. By the end of $2020,$ the number of reservations per night decreased to $75\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{9}.$ What does the factor $75$ mean in this context?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508967 1072 #uuid "a99e73a5-2dae-5b2f-a4fd-53ba3d7abecd" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508967 1074 "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot {b}^{t}$, where we have the following:\n\n- $a=75$ represents the initial number of reservations per night.\n\n- $b=\\frac{3}{4}$ is the decay factor.\n\n- $t=9$ is the time, in months, since March $2020.$\n\nSince $a=75,$ this indicates that there were $75$ reservations initially." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508824 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508889 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508894 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508968 1069 "q-337526" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508968 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508968 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y=16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508968 1072 #uuid "adce0112-6117-576f-afb6-900e0d57f067" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508968 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{4x})}^{′}-4(u{e}^{4x}) & =16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4u{e}^{4x}-4u{e}^{4x} & =16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}} & =16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x} & =16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =16{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x=\\frac{{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =16{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\  & =16{e}^{-4x}(\\frac{{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}}{2}) \\\\  & =8{e}^{-4x}({e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}) \\\\  & =8-8{e}^{-8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =8-8{e}^{-8x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 8-8{e}^{-8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =8x+{e}^{-8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{4x} \\\\  & =8x{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508969 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508840 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508969 1069 "q-315941" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508969 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508969 1071 "![](images/be/be50f6313cb20e06c3d850a3e81f267ac5e2cdc5c2a308280ba6367c08d66fec.png)\n\nMatt is planning a field whose surface is made up of a rectangle and two semicircles, as shown in the diagram. The area of the field must be $9\\,{km}^{2}.$ What should be the radius $r$ if Matt wants a field with the smallest possible perimeter? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{km}$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508969 1072 #uuid "b59b20a8-8e7c-533f-bc4e-e028196934bf" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508969 1074 "We let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector (the same for both semicircles) and let $x$ be the other side of the rectangle. Then, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2x+\\pi r+\\pi r \\\\  & =2x+2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $9$ square units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n9=2rx+\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $2x$ the subject of the constraint equation and taking into account that $r>0,$ we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =2rx+\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 2rx & =9-\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{9}{r}-\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{9}{r}-\\pi r+2\\pi r \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{r}+\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. Computing the derivative gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(r) & =-\\frac{9}{{r}^{2}}+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{9}{{r}^{2}}+\\pi  & =0 \\\\ \\pi  & =\\frac{9}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{\\pi } \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{\\pi }} \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative ${P}^{″}(r)=\\frac{18}{{r}^{3}}>0,$ the stationary point $r=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,\\text{km}$ is a minimum." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508822 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508970 1069 "q-315610" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508970 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508970 1071 "![](images/d8/d85a061ab65d269f22f589e7f57b07a6d474db5f6f29c8c8b412701b99de5241.png)\n\nA box has a square base with side lengths $x$ and height $y,$ as shown above. The surface area of the box is $294$ square units. Find the value of $x$ that maximizes the volume of the box. Do not round the answer.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} units" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508970 1072 #uuid "b92c6e01-0c5a-5f6e-a138-545af5c6ad5d" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508970 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV={x}^{2}y\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The surface area is $294$ square units. So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\,\\text{(area of the base)}+4\\,\\text{(area of one side)} \\\\ 294 & =2{x}^{2}+4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{147}{2x}-\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=\\frac{147}{2x}-\\frac{x}{2}$ in the expression $V={x}^{2}y,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(x) & ={x}^{2}(\\frac{147}{2x}-\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{147}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(x)=\\frac{147}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{147}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =49 \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the side length $x$ must be positive. \n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=7$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(x)=-3x\\,⟹\\,{V}^{″}(7)=-21<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(7)$ is negative, we conclude that $x=7$ is indeed a maximum of $V(x).$\n\nTherefore, the box with the largest possible volume has side length $x=7$ units." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508971 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508901 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508971 1069 "q-117181" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508971 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508971 1071 "![](images/c3/c3f298b4df4e0c8ff66ad9da970c16ab11839f014519fd451dfba050500d9513.png)\n\nA family is having a picnic in a park. They place a big square blanket on the ground. The dimensions of the blanket are shown in the figure above. What is the area covered by the blanket?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508971 1072 #uuid "bbbaba03-02d6-5c29-9b46-9da0f0be8cbb" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508971 1074 "The area of a square with sides of length $a$ is given by \n\n$$\nA={a}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the figure, we obtain that $a=7\\,ft.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={a}^{2}={7}^{2}=49\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508972 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508848 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508972 1069 "q-112525" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508972 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508972 1071 "The caffeine level, in milligrams, in a patient's bloodstream $t$ hours after drinking a cup of coffee is given by the function $C(t)=140\\cdot {(0.9)}^{t}.$ How much caffeine is metabolized between the second and third hour after drinking the coffee?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508972 1072 #uuid "bce8526b-2382-5fd4-882b-274b4364bd61" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508972 1074 "We need to find the level of caffeine in the blood between $t=2$ and $t=3$ hours after drinking a cup of coffee. To calculate this, we find the level of caffeine in the blood after $2$ hours, and we subtract from it the level of caffeine in the blood after $3$ hours. So we need to evaluate\n\n$$\nC(2)-C(3).\n$$\n\nThe level of caffeine in the blood after $2$ hours is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(2) & =140\\cdot {(0.9)}^{2} \\\\  & =140\\cdot 0.81 \\\\  & =113.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe level of caffeine in the blood after $3$ hours is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(3) & =140\\cdot {(0.9)}^{3} \\\\  & =140\\cdot 0.729 \\\\  & =102.06.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the level of caffeine metabolized between $t=2$ and $t=3$ hours after drinking a cup of coffee is\n\n$$\n113.4-102.06=11.34.\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508811 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508973 1069 "q-171954" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508973 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508973 1071 "![](images/f2/f2c8ddcb1cddce59fbecf25ea606e8400d04d896dfdb263dd0aaf4e6c7de682f.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is{{field-1}}\n${cm}^{2}.$\n(Figure not drawn to scale)" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508973 1072 #uuid "c171c6db-2b08-53c8-ae94-2e1ffe80b16e" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508973 1074 "The area of the triangle is defined as \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n where $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the base is \n\n$$\nb=AB=12\\,\\text{cm}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding height is \n\n$$\nh=6\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 6}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{72}{2} \\\\  & =36\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508974 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508852 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508974 1069 "q-345713" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508974 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508974 1071 "![](images/18/1859fcfa509ac8b8c93feed744df6fb66e87fc318811d75ad3cd154c6ab1bb96.png)\n\nConsider a box with a surface area of $196$ square units whose height $y$ is three halves its width $x.$ Find the value of $y$ that maximizes the volume of the box. Do not round the answer.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}} units" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508974 1072 #uuid "c4e37c29-e44f-56ff-9916-25bc220edbdb" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508974 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $y$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=x\\cdot y\\cdot z\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\nSince $y=\\frac{3}{2}x,$ we have $x=\\frac{2}{3}y,$ so\n\n$$\nV=(\\frac{2}{3}y)\\cdot y\\cdot z=\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{2}z.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The surface area is $196$ square units. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xy+2xz+2yz \\\\ 196 & =2xy+2xz+2yz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $x=\\frac{2}{3}y,$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}196 & =2(\\frac{2}{3}y)y+2(\\frac{2}{3}y)z+2yz \\\\ 196 & =\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}yz+2yz \\\\ 196 & =\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{2}+\\frac{10}{3}yz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{294}{5y}-\\frac{2}{5}y\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into the expression $V=\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{2}z$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(y) & =\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{2}(\\frac{294}{5y}-\\frac{2}{5}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{196}{5}y-\\frac{4}{15}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=\\frac{196}{5}-\\frac{4}{5}{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{196}{5}-\\frac{4}{5}{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 196-4{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =49 \\\\ y & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the side length $y$ must be positive.\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $y=7$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=-\\frac{8}{5}y\\,⟹\\,{V}^{″}(7)=-\\frac{56}{5}<0,\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(7)$ is negative, we conclude that $y=7$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the box with the largest possible volume has height $y=7$ units." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508975 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508861 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508975 1069 "q-164404" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508975 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508975 1071 "What is the trace in the $xy$-plane of the parabolic cylinder $(y-4)^{2}=z+1?$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508975 1072 #uuid "c57b9d4a-351a-58d6-893f-d761a9a6ee5e" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508975 1074 "To find the trace in the $xy$-plane, we substitute $z=0$ into the equation of the cylinder, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =0+1 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =1 \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ y & =4\\pm 1 \\\\ y & =3,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of two lines $y=3$ and $y=5$ in the $xy$-plane." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508882 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508976 1069 "q-118721" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508976 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508976 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2})}^{x-1}.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508976 1072 #uuid "c8a37d3e-d129-5ae7-8ba8-cbd709bbd8bf" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508976 1074 "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2})}^{x-1}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2})}^{x-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{(2x-2)+1}{2x-2})}^{x-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{2x-2})}^{x-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{2(x-1)})}^{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $2(x-1)=n$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x-1) & =n \\\\ x-1 & =\\frac{n}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2})}^{x-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{2(x-1)})}^{x-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n/2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508977 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508836 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508977 1069 "q-95111" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508977 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508977 1071 "Given that $q(x,y)=xy,$ find $\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508977 1072 #uuid "c9126714-6e57-5593-b5b4-4801efbfb113" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508977 1074 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $q(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂q}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂q}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂q}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂q}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $q$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂q}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂x} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂q}{∂y} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508978 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508893 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508978 1069 "q-315627" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508978 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508978 1071 "![](images/a9/a9de332f316c91f9739208d11828f6a0f7d035589dc4f3adc921e5108453cc12.png)\n\nA box with no lid and a square base has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the base costs $$5$ per square meter, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$2$ per square meter. The box must have a volume of $10\\,{m}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $x$ for the box that has the smallest production cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} units" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508978 1072 #uuid "caab3419-40f4-5f42-9fc6-7d0c53c70e6a" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508978 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =5{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4xy \\\\  & =5{x}^{2}+8xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $10\\,{m}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot x\\cdot y \\\\ 10 & ={x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =10 \\\\ y & =\\frac{10}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =5{x}^{2}+8xy \\\\  & =5{x}^{2}+8x\\cdot \\frac{10}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =5{x}^{2}+\\frac{80}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(x)=10x-\\frac{80}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 10x-\\frac{80}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 10{x}^{3} & =80 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =8 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=2$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(x)=10+\\frac{160}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(2)=10+20=30>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(2)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=2$ is indeed a minimum of $C(x).$\n\nTherefore, the production cost is the smallest when $x=2$ units." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508979 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508900 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508979 1069 "q-338566" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508979 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508979 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{3}{x})}^{2x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508979 1072 #uuid "cff12c80-2e8b-52fc-a40b-42f5ea296e0f" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508979 1074 "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{3}{x})}^{2x}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nIn the given limit, we let $\\frac{3}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}.$ Thus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n} \\\\ x & =3n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{3}{x})}^{2x} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2(3n)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{6} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{6} \\\\  & ={e}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508980 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508908 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508980 1069 "q-64120" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508980 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508980 1071 "The value of a boat, in dollars, depreciates according to the function $p(t)=10\\,000\\cdot {(0.9)}^{t},$ where $t$ is the number of years since the boat was purchased. Calculate $p(4),$ and determine its meaning in this context." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508980 1072 #uuid "d33dc350-97af-5862-9547-76e026eaf801" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508980 1074 "The value $p(4)$ represents the value of $p(t)$ at $t=4.$ In this context, it represents the value of the boat $4$ years after it was purchased.\n\nSubstituting $t=4$ into the expression for $p(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(4) & =10\\,000\\cdot {(0.9)}^{4} \\\\  & =10\\,000\\cdot (0.656\\,1) \\\\  & =6\\,561.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the boat after four years is $$6,561.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508981 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508844 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508981 1069 "q-86451" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508981 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508981 1071 "![](images/cf/cf7c07babafa92e2c88e2e08727edc0d724b05f1318f6cf022d9f8b31ff668d7.png)\n\nA piece of cardboard measures $15\\,in\\times 8\\,in.$ A tray of height $y\\,\\text{in}$ is formed by cutting a $y\\times y\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}$ square from each of the corners and folding up the sides, as shown in the diagram. Determine the height of the box that gives the maximum possible volume." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508981 1072 #uuid "d416390a-8b08-58ae-b4bd-805af1c30ba3" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508981 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x,y,$ and $z$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the width $x$ and the depth $z$ of the tray are given by\n\n$$\nx=15-2y,\\,z=8-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0$ and $z>0,$ we must have that $15-2y>0$ and $8-2y>0$, hence $y<4.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<4.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =xyz \\\\  & =(15-2y)(y)(8-2y) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-46{y}^{2}+120y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-92y+120,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-92y+120 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-23y+30 & =0 \\\\ (3y-5)(y-6) & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{3},\\,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $6$ since we already know that $0<y<4.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-92.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(\\frac{5}{3})$ is negative, we conclude that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the stationary point $y=\\frac{5}{3}$ gives the maximum possible volume." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508982 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508846 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508982 1069 "q-112129" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508982 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508982 1071 "A five gallons water bottle is leaking its content. The number of gallons of water left inside the bottle after $t$ minutes is given by the formula $W(t)=5\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{t}.$ How many gallons of water will be inside the bottle after $3$ minutes?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508982 1072 #uuid "d4c0de90-e894-5c05-811c-ac221194e0c5" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508982 1074 "To get the number of gallons of water inside the bottle after $3$ minutes, we evaluate the function at $t=3.$ Substituting $t=3$ into the function $W(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =5\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{3} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{125} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{125} \\\\  & =0.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, after $3$ minutes, there will be $0.04$ gallons of water inside the bottle." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508983 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508881 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508983 1069 "q-326544" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508983 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508983 1071 "An office chair is marked down by $15%$ during a promotion. What is the sale price of the chair if its original price is $$85?$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508983 1072 #uuid "d7719fa6-fde6-59d2-a1a3-a59122c8717b" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508983 1074 "To find an amount that's $15%$ *less than* $$85,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%-15%=85%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n85%=\\frac{85}{100}=0.85\n$$\n\nSo, to find $85%$ of $$85,$ we multiply $$85$ by $0.85:$\n\n$$\n0.85\\cdot $85=$72.25\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sale price of the chair is $$72.25.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508819 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508984 1069 "q-17957" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508984 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508984 1071 "![](images/0d/0da2ed0bca11725e7ffb3fa3c5952807e406530a0625b01ebb476f0145b64e30.png)\n\nA farmer wants to fence a field that is made up of a rectangle and two semicircles, as shown in the diagram. He has only $200\\,m$ of fencing. What is the length $2x$ of the side of the field that covers the greatest area?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508984 1072 #uuid "d85466ba-cd37-5739-bfc9-c486bbfacc9a" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508984 1074 "First,  let $r$ the radius of the upper semicircle (top) and $x$ the radius of the lateral semicircle (right), hence the dimensions of the rectangle are $2r$ and $2x.$ We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =(2r)(2x)+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi {x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4rx+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi {x}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $200$ units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n200=\\pi r+\\pi x+2x+2r\\,.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $x$ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}200 & =\\pi r+\\pi x+2x+2r \\\\ x(\\pi +2) & =200-r(\\pi +2) \\\\ x & =\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4rx+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi {x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4r(\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}{(\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A,$ so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =4(\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)+4r(-1)+\\pi r+\\pi (\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{800}{\\pi +2}-4r-4r+\\pi r-\\frac{200\\pi }{\\pi +2}+\\pi r \\\\  & =-8r+2\\pi r+\\frac{800-200\\pi }{\\pi +2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -8r+2\\pi r+\\frac{800-200\\pi }{\\pi +2} & =0 \\\\ 2r(\\pi -4) & =-\\frac{800-200\\pi }{\\pi +2} \\\\ 2r(\\pi -4) & =\\frac{200\\pi -800}{\\pi +2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{100\\pi -400}{(\\pi +2)(\\pi -4)} \\\\ r & =\\frac{100(\\pi -4)}{(\\pi +2)(\\pi -4)} \\\\ r & =\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we confirm that $r=\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-8+2\\pi \\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{100}{\\pi +2})=-8+2\\pi <0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{100}{\\pi +2})$ is negative, we conclude that $r=\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nFinally, when $r=\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-\\frac{100}{\\pi +2} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the side of the field with the largest area is $2x=\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508985 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508818 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508985 1069 "q-3478" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508985 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508985 1071 "![](images/31/31df5fe79f2aa8715321b6500474683a01732788e5fafc3300dfffc83c266893.png)\n\nA box whose width $x$ is $3$ times its depth $z$ has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the top and bottom costs $$5$ per square foot, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$2$ per square foot. The box must have a volume of $50\\,{ft}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $z$ for the box that has the smallest production cost?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508985 1072 #uuid "d9422237-5095-57b7-8cd1-109d7184ed3e" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508985 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $z$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =5\\cdot 2xz+2\\cdot 2xy+2\\cdot 2yz \\\\  & =10xz+4xy+4yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $50\\,{ft}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot z\\cdot y \\\\ 50 & =3{z}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}y & =50 \\\\ y & =\\frac{50}{3{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =10xz+4xy+4yz \\\\ C & =10(3z)z+4(3z)(\\frac{50}{3{z}^{2}})+4(\\frac{50}{3{z}^{2}})z \\\\ C & =30{z}^{2}+\\frac{200}{z}+\\frac{200}{3z} \\\\ C & =30{z}^{2}+\\frac{800}{3z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(z)=60z-\\frac{800}{3{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ 60z-\\frac{800}{3{z}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 180{z}^{3} & =800 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =\\frac{800}{180} \\\\ z & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(z)=60+\\frac{1\\,600}{{z}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}})>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}})$ is positive, we conclude that $z=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}}$ is indeed a minimum of $C(z).$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508986 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508910 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508986 1069 "q-53230" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508986 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508986 1071 "![](images/47/473b8a18bb4d2bf77cfe18eb735bcb64ec95a551faadb18ad38f561c81d3b99c.png)\n\nFind the area of the rectangle $ABCD$ shown above." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508986 1072 #uuid "da7691ce-ed2d-5165-8a4d-fdf1f9bd503b" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508986 1074 "The area of a rectangle is given by \n\n$$\nA=a\\cdot b,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are the lengths of the sides.\n\nFrom the figure, we obtain that \n\n$$\na=AB=9\\,cm,\\,b=AD=5\\,cm.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the area of the rectangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =a\\cdot b=9\\cdot 5=45\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508987 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508860 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508987 1069 "q-97505" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508987 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508987 1071 "Given that $X∼B(7,0.4),$ compute $P(X\\le 2).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508987 1072 #uuid "dda52838-e549-5595-ba17-f5adb7013bf8" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508987 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,0.4),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.4)^{x}(0.6)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{0})(0.4)^{0}(0.6)^{7-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{1})(0.4)^{1}(0.6)^{7-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{2})(0.4)^{2}(0.6)^{7-2} \\\\  & =1(0.4)^{0}(0.6)^{7}+7(0.4)(0.6)^{6}+21(0.4)^{2}(0.6)^{5} \\\\  & ≈0.4199\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508988 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508899 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508988 1069 "q-346760" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508988 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508988 1071 "![](images/6f/6f753b0f958204f705f0d59610210ae20e8f3096f0a9847bf72e2d3ab43a6305.png)\n\nConsider a box with width $x,$ height $y,$ and depth $z,$ as shown above. The depth of the box is half its width. Given that the box's volume is $243$ cubic units, what is the smallest possible surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ of the box? Do not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} square units" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508988 1072 #uuid "e2adf7b9-c049-5411-a710-9c822b654299" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508988 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is $243$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 243 & =xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $z=\\frac{x}{2},$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n243=xy(\\frac{x}{2})=\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{2} & =243 \\\\ {x}^{2}y & =486 \\\\ y & =\\frac{486}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2x(\\frac{x}{2})+2x(\\frac{486}{{x}^{2}})+2(\\frac{486}{{x}^{2}})(\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{972}{x}+\\frac{486}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{1458}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=2x-\\frac{1458}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{1458}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{1458}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 2{x}^{3} & =1458 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =729 \\\\ x & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=9$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(x)=2+\\frac{2916}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(9)=2+4=6>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(9)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=9$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(9) & ={9}^{2}+\\frac{1458}{9} \\\\  & =243\\,\\text{square units}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508989 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508835 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508989 1069 "q-26244" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508989 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508989 1071 "A pest controller was responsible for humanely reducing the number of mice within a hotel. If there were $720$ mice initially and $\\frac{5}{6}$ of mice were removed from the hotel every day, how many mice remained in the hotel after two days?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508989 1072 #uuid "e2edca84-13da-5da7-b4b8-ed4b37f13789" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508989 1074 "Since the number of mice reduced by $\\frac{5}{6}$ per day, then only $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the mice from the previous day remained.\n\nSo we have:\n\n- At the start, there were $720$ mice.\n\n- After $1$ day, there were $720\\cdot (\\frac{1}{6})$ mice.\n\n- After $2$ days, there were $720\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}$ mice.\n\nEvaluating the last expression, we get\n\n$$\n720\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}=20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $2$ days only $20$ mice remained." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508890 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508990 1069 "q-106807" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508990 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508990 1071 "Let $P$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices $A=({a}_{ij})$ such that ${a}_{22}=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. For any $A,B\\in P$ and any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (A+B)=\\alpha A+\\alpha B.$\n\nII. The scalar multiplication of matrices in $P$ is associative.\n\nIII. For any $A\\in P,$ we have $1\\cdot A={I}_{2}.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508990 1072 #uuid "e4e1e0d5-3773-5038-a8b4-577c1254dd92" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508990 1074 "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrices $A=({a}_{ij})$ and $B=({b}_{ij})$ from $P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha ({a}_{ij}+{b}_{ij})=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\alpha {b}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha (A+B)=\\alpha A+\\alpha B.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matric $A=({a}_{ij})$ from $P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta {a}_{ij})=(\\alpha \\beta ){a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta A)=(\\alpha \\beta )A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ from $P.$ Then,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot 0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\ne {I}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508991 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508897 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508991 1069 "q-21055" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508991 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508991 1071 "![](images/b1/b12eaaaf9aafa591bef00ca1b11d24783fae4a46f7253bddb05b9b206dbcb9e6.png)\n\nFind $HB$ given that the area of $△ABC$ is $140\\,{m}^{2},$  $AH=15\\,m,$ and $CH=14\\,m.$\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508991 1072 #uuid "e5a09c22-28d0-55d4-9b16-abdb75945ec5" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508991 1074 "The area of the triangle is defined as \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n where $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the base is $b=AB,$ and the corresponding height is \n\n$$\nh=CH=14\\,m.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the formula for the area, we can solve for the base as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 140 & =\\frac{b\\cdot 14}{2} \\\\ 280 & =b\\cdot 14 \\\\ b & =\\frac{280}{14} \\\\ b & =20\\,m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $AB=20\\,m$ and hence, we have\n\n$$\nHB=AB-AH=20-15=5\\,m.\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508992 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508813 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508992 1069 "q-327179" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508992 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508992 1071 "What number is $15%$ less than $100$?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508992 1072 #uuid "e8cd627c-513a-5c63-8a84-0bf723703c1a" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508992 1074 "To find a number $15%$ *less than* $100,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%-15%=85%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n85%=\\frac{85}{100}=0.85\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $85%$ of $100,$ we multiply $100$ by $0.85:$\n\n$$\n0.85\\cdot 100=85\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508869 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508993 1069 "q-164405" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508993 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508993 1071 "Identify the surface $z=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508993 1072 #uuid "ec111185-2cdb-5c2e-acc4-77d77fc0a299" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508993 1074 "Recall that the equation of an elliptic paraboloid centered at $O$ is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is an elliptic paraboloid with $a=\\sqrt{2},\\,b=1.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508994 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508903 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508994 1069 "q-62062" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508994 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508994 1071 "Joel invested $$11\\,280.$ The investment performed poorly, losing one-eighth of its value every month.\n\nThe function $p(t)$ models the value of the investment after $t$ months and is given by\n\n$$\np(t)=11\\,280\\cdot {(\\text{___})}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWhat number is missing?" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508994 1072 #uuid "ec5e8c7c-c10d-59da-bddc-7ee7a7739de6" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508994 1074 "The initial investment was $$11\\,280$, and this decreased by $\\frac{1}{8}$ every month.\n\nIf the investment value is reduced by $\\frac{1}{8},$ then $\\frac{7}{8}$ of its value remained every month. So the decay factor is $\\frac{7}{8}.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay factor $b$) is\n\n$$\np=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=11\\,280$ is the initial investment.\n\n- $b=\\frac{7}{8}$ is the decay factor.\n\n- $t=$ the time, in months.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $p(t)$ is\n\n$$\np(t)=11\\,280\\cdot {(\\frac{7}{8})}^{t}.\n$$\n\nSo the missing number is $\\frac{7}{8}.$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508851 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508995 1069 "q-276035" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508995 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508995 1071 "If $X∼B(4,\\frac{1}{3}),$  then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(1\\le X<3)≈${{field-1}} ." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508995 1072 #uuid "efd53794-367f-547c-a3cb-0e0a7df2cfac" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508995 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(4,\\frac{1}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X<3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-2} \\\\  & =4{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}+6{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.691\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508996 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508876 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508996 1069 "q-117368" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508996 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508996 1071 "![](images/b6/b66dea0994329cf63ff08137293efc33fe994a5ae0395d661dc014044a1c0d14.png)\n\nConsider the triangles $△ABC$ and $△CDE$ shown above. If the area of $△CDE$ is $27{\\text{ cm}}^{2}$, find the area of $△ABC.$\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508996 1072 #uuid "efe752cf-6c60-5a23-aae8-55bc453cac9b" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508996 1074 "Let's consider $△CDE.$ We can see that $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is the height corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{DE}$ and that $DE=6\\text{ cm}.$ \n\nSince the area of $△CDE$ is $27{\\text{ cm}}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 27 & =\\frac{6\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\ 6CD & =54 \\\\ CD & =9\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is also the height of $△ABC$ corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n![](images/95/95e84873b1353e6a04bb4e6e7df5f45c09eb4751b1170d6eaf8a9bcfa4b572de.png)\n\nTherefore, we find the area of $△ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{AB\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 9}{2} \\\\  & =18{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508997 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508877 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508997 1069 "q-129982" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508997 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508997 1071 "Given that $X∼B(6,0.6),$ compute $P(X\\ge 2).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508997 1072 #uuid "f971c3b7-c38e-56ce-b09c-6e2a701c1e91" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508997 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,0.6),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.6)^{x}(0.4)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X\\ge 2)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=2,3,4,5,6.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X<2)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X<2),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{0})(0.6)^{0}(0.4)^{6-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{1})(0.6)^{1}(0.4)^{6-1} \\\\  & =1(0.6)^{0}(0.4)^{6}+6(0.6)^{1}(0.4)^{5} \\\\  & ≈0.0410\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & ≈1-0.0410 \\\\  & =0.9590\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508998 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508891 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508998 1069 "q-117300" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508998 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508998 1071 "![](images/dd/dd5731dcea0cc6bc8ffc0aad963f4b05b57a7422512a2df3df64666e63254f77.png)\n\nA piece of cardboard measures $10\\,in\\times 10\\,in.$ A tray of height $y\\,\\text{in}$ is formed by cutting a $y\\times y\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}$ square from each of the corners and folding up the sides, as shown in the diagram. Determine the height of the box that gives the maximum possible volume." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508998 1072 #uuid "fcc5cb30-3a54-5c1c-aecb-10ee16fa7222" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508998 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV={x}^{2}y\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the side length $x$ of the tray is given by\n\n$$\nx=10-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0,$ we must have that $10-2y>0$ hence $y<5.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<5.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{2}y \\\\  & =(10-2y)^{2}y \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-40{y}^{2}+100y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-80y+100,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-80y+100 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-20y+25 & =0 \\\\ (3y-5)(y-5) & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{3},\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $5$ since we already know that $0<y<5.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-80.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(\\frac{5}{3})=-40$ is negative, we conclude that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the stationary point $y=\\frac{5}{3}$ gives the maximum possible volume." 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508884 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508999 1069 "q-164478" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508999 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508999 1071 "Identify the following surface: \n\n$$\n25(x-1)^{2}+16(y-2)^{2}=1,\\,-\\infty <z<\\infty \n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508999 1072 #uuid "fcd8e2d4-6c09-5241-9d34-1a3c5fb626c5" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186508999 1074 "Recall that the equation of an elliptic cylinder, whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nand an elliptic cylinder, whose axis of symmetry is parallel to the $z$-axis and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ in the $xy$-plane, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is an elliptic cylinder with the following parameters:\n\n$$\n({x}_{0},{y}_{0})=(1,2),\\,a=\\frac{1}{5},\\,b=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186509000 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508859 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186509000 1069 "q-123874" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186509000 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186509000 1071 "![](images/9c/9c18d92044cb10ba7096ff66c14a27b3972fce71ef682e595bfda6fe17d3627d.png)\n\nUsing the graphical method, find the solutions $(x,y)$ of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=5 \\\\ y=9-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592186509000 1072 #uuid "fd6b1704-ef29-5746-9fcc-ddfee319c814" 13194139535463 17592186045417 true] [17592